editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
Binary search needs **monotonicity**, not sortedness. A sorted array is just the most familiar monotone predicate (`a[i] >= x`).

## Approach
Identical to lower bound with `a[mid] < x` replaced by `f(mid) == false`:

```java
int lo = 0, hi = n;
while (lo < hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (!f(mid)) lo = mid + 1;
    else         hi = mid;
}
return lo < n ? lo : -1;
```

## Why it matters later
"Smallest capacity that ships in D days", "smallest speed that finishes in time", "largest minimum gap" — all of them are this loop with `f` replaced by a feasibility check. The hard part of those problems is never the search; it is noticing that feasibility is monotone.

