editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
The outer loop. A single BFS explores one component; *counting* components needs the `for (s = 0; s < n; s++)` wrapper around it, and forgetting that wrapper is the most common way this is got wrong — it silently reports 1 on a disconnected graph.

## Approach
```java
boolean[] seen = new boolean[n];
int comps = 0;
for (int s = 0; s < n; s++) {
    if (seen[s]) continue;
    comps++;
    seen[s] = true;
    Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
    q.add(s);
    while (!q.isEmpty()) {
        int u = q.poll();
        for (int v : g.get(u)) if (!seen[v]) { seen[v] = true; q.add(v); }
    }
}
```

**Mark on enqueue.** If you instead mark when you dequeue, a node reachable from two already-queued nodes gets added twice. On a small graph that is merely wasteful; on a dense one it is exponential blow-up, and the symptom is a timeout rather than a wrong answer.

A self-loop and a repeated edge are both harmless here precisely because `seen` is checked before enqueueing — worth confirming on the hidden tests rather than special-casing.

