editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**When the answer does not depend on order, choose the order.** A sum over all pairs is the same whichever way the array is arranged, and sorted is the arrangement where `|a[j] − a[i]|` loses its absolute value.

## Approach
After sorting, every earlier element is ≤ `a[j]`, so the pairs ending at `j` contribute

`(a[j] − a[0]) + (a[j] − a[1]) + … + (a[j] − a[j−1]) = a[j]·j − (a[0] + … + a[j−1])`

```java
Arrays.sort(a);
long before = 0, total = 0;
for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
    total += (long) a[j] * j - before;
    before += a[j];
}
```

## Pricing it
The brute force is Θ(n²) — 5·10⁹ pairs at the limit. The sort costs O(n log n) and the rest is the pair-products pass again. Paying n log n to make an n² problem linear is one of the most common trades there is, and it is only allowed because the question is about the *set* of values, not their positions.

## Overflow
`a[j] * j` is up to 10⁶ · 10⁵ = 10¹¹, so cast before multiplying. The total is at most about 5·10¹⁵, comfortably inside a `long`.

