editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**A comparator is a list of tie-breaks.** Compare the most important key; only if it is equal, compare the next.

```java
players.sort((a, b) -> {
    if (a.score != b.score) return Integer.compare(b.score, a.score);  // high first
    return a.name.compareTo(b.name);                                  // then A→Z
});
```

or, with the library: `Comparator.comparingInt((P p) -> -p.score).thenComparing(p -> p.name)`.

## Assigning ranks
Walk the sorted list. The rank is `i + 1`, except when the score equals the previous player's, in which case it stays what it was:

```java
int rank = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (i == 0 || players.get(i).score != players.get(i - 1).score) rank = i + 1;
    System.out.println(rank + " " + players.get(i).name + " " + players.get(i).score);
}
```

Using `rank + 1` instead of `i + 1` gives *dense* ranking (1, 2, 2, 3) — a different, also common, convention. Read which one the statement asks for.

## The bug to avoid
`return b.score - a.score` works for these limits and is a habit that overflows the day scores can be negative or near `Integer.MAX_VALUE`. `Integer.compare` costs nothing and never overflows.

