editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**A stack evaluates what nesting describes.** Reverse Polish notation needs no brackets and no precedence rules, because the order of the tokens already says which operation happens first — and the stack is the machine that reads that order.

## Approach
```java
Deque<Long> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
for (String t : tokens) {
    switch (t) {
        case "+": { long b = st.pop(), a = st.pop(); st.push(a + b); break; }
        case "-": { long b = st.pop(), a = st.pop(); st.push(a - b); break; }
        case "*": { long b = st.pop(), a = st.pop(); st.push(a * b); break; }
        case "/": { long b = st.pop(), a = st.pop(); st.push(a / b); break; }
        default:  st.push(Long.parseLong(t));
    }
}
System.out.println(st.pop());
```

## The two bugs
- **Operand order.** `b` is popped first because it was pushed last, so `4 13 5 /` is `13 / 5`, not `5 / 13`. Addition and multiplication hide this bug; subtraction and division expose it.
- **Negative numbers.** Testing `t.charAt(0) == '-'` to detect the operator misreads `-11`. Compare the whole token.

## Where this goes next
Converting ordinary infix (`(2 + 1) * 3`) into this form is the shunting-yard algorithm — a second stack, of operators. A calculator is those two stacks run one after the other.

