editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Validity as a formula.** A window needs `length − maxCount` changes to become one letter, so "at most k changes" becomes a budget the sliding window can maintain, like the zero-flips problem with the most-frequent letter playing the part of the 1s.

## Approach
```java
int[] count = new int[26];
int left = 0, maxf = 0, best = 0;
for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
    maxf = Math.max(maxf, ++count[s.charAt(r) - 'A']);
    while (r - left + 1 - maxf > k) count[s.charAt(left++) - 'A']--;
    best = Math.max(best, r - left + 1);
}
```

## Why a stale `maxf` is fine
Shrinking the window can lower the true top count, and `maxf` is never decreased. That can only make the window look *more* valid than it is — but `best` only grows when a window of a new length is reached with that `maxf`, and `maxf` was genuinely achieved by some earlier window of that length or less. So no invalid length is ever recorded. Recomputing the max over 26 counts each step is also O(n), if you would rather not rely on the argument.

