editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**The count-all-windows shape with a product.** Positivity plays the role non-negativity plays for sums: extending a window never makes the product smaller, so the valid left ends for a fixed right end are a contiguous range, and `left` never has to move back.

## Approach
```java
if (k <= 1) return 0;
long prod = 1, count = 0;
int left = 0;
for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
    prod *= a[r];
    while (prod >= k) prod /= a[left++];
    count += r - left + 1;
}
```

## The k ≤ 1 guard
Every product is at least 1, so with `k ≤ 1` nothing qualifies — and without the guard the `while` divides the window empty and then keeps going, `left` running past `r` and dividing by elements that were never multiplied in.

## Why division is exact
`prod` is always the product of exactly `a[left..r]`, so dividing by `a[left]` is exact integer division. The same trick with sums would be subtraction; with a product that might contain 0 it would be undefined — which is why the statement says *positive*.

