editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Greedy matching is safe for subsequences.** If `s[i]` can be matched at several positions of `t`, matching it at the *earliest* leaves the most of `t` for the rest of `s`, so there is never a reason to wait.

## Approach
```java
int i = 0;
for (int j = 0; j < t.length() && i < s.length(); j++)
    if (s.charAt(i) == t.charAt(j)) i++;
return i == s.length() ? "YES" : "NO";
```

## The follow-up
With a *billion* different `s` queries against one `t`, one pass per query is too slow. Precompute, for each letter, the sorted list of its positions in `t`, and match each character of `s` by binary-searching for the first position after the previous match: O(|s| log |t|) per query.

