## The one thing this teaches
**Tokenising is where string bugs live.** Reversing a list of words is trivial; producing that list correctly from `"  the sky   is blue "` is the actual problem.

## Approach — scan from the end
```java
StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
int i = s.length() - 1;
while (i >= 0) {
    while (i >= 0 && s.charAt(i) == ' ') i--;       // skip spaces
    if (i < 0) break;
    int end = i;
    while (i >= 0 && s.charAt(i) != ' ') i--;       // find the word's start
    if (out.length() > 0) out.append(' ');
    out.append(s, i + 1, end + 1);
}
```

## Why `split(" ")` fails
`"a  b".split(" ")` is `["a", "", "b"]`: the empty token between two spaces survives, and joining gives two spaces. `split("\\s+")` treats a run as one separator, but a *leading* run still yields a leading empty token — which is why the `trim()` comes first.

## The in-place version
With a mutable character array: reverse the whole array, then reverse each word back. O(1) extra space, and the classic follow-up.

