## The one thing this teaches
**Equal remainders, not equal sums.** `(prefix[j] − prefix[i]) mod k == 0` is the same statement as `prefix[j] ≡ prefix[i] (mod k)`, so the "subarray sum equals K" technique carries over with the map keyed by remainder.

## Approach
```java
long[] seen = new long[k];
seen[0] = 1;                 // the empty prefix
long run = 0, count = 0;
for (int x : a) {
    run += x;
    int r = Math.floorMod(run, k);
    count += seen[r];
    seen[r]++;
}
```

## The negative remainder
Java's `%` keeps the sign of the dividend, so a running sum of −3 has remainder −3 while one of 2 has remainder 2 — the same class mod 5, stored in two different slots, and never counted as a pair. With negatives in the input this is the only bug that matters. `Math.floorMod` always returns a value in `[0, k)`.

