## The one thing this teaches
**A stack of survivors — and when you can do without it.** Reading forwards, a `#` erases the top of what you have typed, so a stack models the editor exactly. Reading *backwards*, you know how many backspaces are pending before you meet the characters they erase, so a counter replaces the stack.

## Approach — from the back
```java
int i = s.length() - 1, j = t.length() - 1;
while (i >= 0 || j >= 0) {
    i = nextKept(s, i);
    j = nextKept(t, j);
    if (i < 0 || j < 0) return i == j ? "YES" : "NO";
    if (s.charAt(i) != t.charAt(j)) return "NO";
    i--; j--;
}
return "YES";
```

`nextKept` walks left from `i`: a `#` adds one to a skip count, a letter with a positive skip count is erased, and the first letter with no skip pending is returned.

## The trap
Comparing as soon as one string runs out: `a#` and `` are equal, and so are `ab##` and `c#d#`. Both pointers must be advanced past erased characters *before* deciding that one side is empty.

