## The one thing this teaches
**A stack of context you can back out of.** Walking into a directory pushes it; `..` leaves the most recent one. That is the same last-in, first-out shape as brackets — only the tokens are words.

## Approach
```java
Deque<String> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
for (String part : path.split("/")) {
    if (part.isEmpty() || part.equals(".")) continue;
    if (part.equals("..")) { if (!st.isEmpty()) st.pollLast(); }
    else st.addLast(part);
}
StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
for (String d : st) out.append('/').append(d);
return out.length() == 0 ? "/" : out.toString();
```

Using the deque's *last* end as the top means iterating it front-to-back yields the path in order — no reversal.

## The cases that decide it
- `..` at the root stays at the root.
- `...` and `..a` are ordinary names: compare whole tokens, never prefixes.
- An empty result is `/`, not the empty string.

