## The one thing this teaches
**A queue is a window over time.** When events arrive in order and expire in the same order, the oldest event is always the next to expire — first in, first out.

## Approach
```java
Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
for (int t : times) {
    q.addLast(t);
    while (q.peekFirst() < t - 3000) q.pollFirst();
    out.add(q.size());
}
```

Amortised O(1) per call: a call can be polled many times *in total* only once.

## Boundaries
The window is inclusive, so a call at exactly `t − 3000` still counts — the condition to expire is `< t − 3000`, not `<=`. With times that were not sorted, the front would not be the oldest and a heap or a sorted structure would be needed.

