## The one thing this teaches
**When the window breaks, keep the candidates that could still win.** With non-negative numbers this is a sliding window. Negatives destroy the monotonicity the window relies on — but a deque of prefix-sum indices restores it by discarding starts that can never be the best.

## Approach
```java
long[] P = new long[n + 1];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i + 1] = P[i] + a[i];
Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();
int best = n + 1;
for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
    while (!dq.isEmpty() && P[j] - P[dq.peekFirst()] >= k) best = Math.min(best, j - dq.pollFirst());
    while (!dq.isEmpty() && P[dq.peekLast()] >= P[j]) dq.pollLast();
    dq.addLast(j);
}
return best <= n ? best : -1;
```

## The two pruning rules
- **Back:** if `i < j` and `P[i] ≥ P[j]`, then `j` is a better start than `i` for every future end — it is later (shorter) and its prefix is no larger (bigger sum). `i` can go.
- **Front:** once `front` pairs validly with `j`, pairing it with any later end is longer. Record it and pop.

Together they keep the deque's prefix sums strictly increasing, which is what lets the front test stop at the first failure.

