## The one thing this teaches
**Flood from the outside, lowest wall first.** Water leaves through the lowest point of the boundary around it. If you always process the lowest wall on the current boundary, then the first time you reach a cell, that wall *is* the lowest barrier between the cell and the outside — the same invariant that makes Dijkstra correct.

## Approach
```java
PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((x, y) -> x[0] - y[0]);   // {height, r, c}
// push every border cell, mark visited
long water = 0;
while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    int[] cur = pq.poll();
    for (each neighbour (nr, nc) not visited) {
        visited[nr][nc] = true;
        water += Math.max(0, cur[0] - h[nr][nc]);
        pq.offer(new int[]{Math.max(cur[0], h[nr][nc]), nr, nc});
    }
}
```

## The key line
The neighbour is pushed with height `max(wall, own height)`: once filled, its water surface acts as a wall for the cells beyond it. Pushing its bare height would let water "leak" through a cell that is itself underwater.

