## The one thing this teaches
**Version history instead of copies.** A snapshot of the whole array is mostly identical to the previous one. Recording only *changes*, tagged with the version they belong to, makes a snapshot free and turns reading an old version into "find the last change at or before this version" — a binary search.

## Approach
```java
List<int[]>[] hist;          // per index: {snapId, value}, ids increasing
int snapId = 0;

void set(int i, int v) {
    List<int[]> h = hist[i];
    if (!h.isEmpty() && h.get(h.size() - 1)[0] == snapId) h.get(h.size() - 1)[1] = v;
    else h.add(new int[]{snapId, v});
}
int snap() { return snapId++; }
int get(int i, int s) {  // last entry with id <= s, else 0
    ... binary search ...
}
```

## Two details
- Sets between the same two snaps collapse into one entry — otherwise a get must find the *last* of several equal ids.
- An index never set before snapshot `s` is 0. Seeding each list with `{-1, 0}` removes that special case.

This is the idea behind persistent data structures and database MVCC: readers of an old version never block, and nothing is copied.

