## The one thing this teaches
**Two traversals, composed.** A DFS or flood fill *identifies* a set; a BFS *measures distance* from a set. Seeding the BFS with every cell of one island at once is what makes it "distance from the island" rather than "distance from one cell" — and it is the difference between O(r·c) and O((r·c)²).

## Approach
```java
// 1. flood fill the first land cell found; mark its cells 2 and add them to q
// 2. BFS by levels
for (int steps = 0; ; steps++) {
    for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {
        int[] cur = q.poll();
        for (each neighbour (x, y)) {
            if (g[x][y] == '1') return steps;   // reached island B
            if (g[x][y] == '0') { g[x][y] = '2'; q.add(new int[]{x, y}); }
        }
    }
}
```

## Counting flips, not steps
From island A, touching island B directly (distance 0 levels of water) means 0 flips; each BFS level crossed over water adds one flipped cell. Returning `steps` when a neighbour is land from B counts exactly the water cells between them.

