## The one thing this teaches
**BFS layers of a topological sort measure depth.** Taking every available course immediately is optimal, because delaying a course can only delay what depends on it. So semester k contains exactly the courses whose longest prerequisite chain has length k — and Kahn's algorithm, run layer by layer, finds them.

## Approach
```java
Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] == 0) q.add(i);
int semesters = 0, taken = 0;
while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    semesters++;
    for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {
        int u = q.poll();
        taken++;
        for (int v : adj.get(u)) if (--indeg[v] == 0) q.add(v);
    }
}
return taken == n ? semesters : -1;
```

## Same answer, other name
The number of semesters is the number of nodes on the longest path in the DAG. The layered BFS computes that without ever tracking paths.

