## The one thing this teaches
**A search that remembers its best guess.** Searching for `k` in a BST walks one path. Every node on that path larger than `k` is a successor candidate, and each later candidate is smaller than the earlier ones (it was found by going left). So the answer is simply the last candidate seen.

## Approach
```java
int succ = -1;
while (root != null) {
    if (root.val > k) { succ = root.val; root = root.left; }
    else root = root.right;
}
return succ;
```

## Why `k` need not be in the tree
The walk never tests equality. It follows the path where `k` *would* be, and the successor of a missing value is the smallest value above where it would sit — the same thing the loop computes.

Swap `>` for `<` and `left` for `right` and it finds the predecessor; replace the strict comparison and it finds a ceiling. `TreeMap.higherKey` is this loop.

