## The one thing this teaches
**Dijkstra needs monotone costs, not sums.** Its proof only uses the fact that extending a path never makes it cheaper. `max(cost, next)` has that property just as `cost + weight` does with non-negative weights, so the same algorithm finds the path whose highest point is lowest.

## Approach
```java
PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);   // {cost, r, c}
pq.add(new int[]{grid[0][0], 0, 0});
seen[0][0] = true;
while (true) {
    int[] cur = pq.poll();
    if (cur[1] == n - 1 && cur[2] == n - 1) return cur[0];
    for (each unseen neighbour (x, y)) {
        seen[x][y] = true;
        pq.add(new int[]{Math.max(cur[0], grid[x][y]), x, y});
    }
}
```

## Two other routes to the same answer
- **Binary search on t**: "can I cross at time t?" is monotone and answered by a BFS, so O(n² log n²).
- **Union–find**: add cells in increasing elevation and stop when the corners join — Kruskal's algorithm stopped early.

The start cell's own elevation counts: you cannot begin before the water reaches it.

