## The one thing this teaches
**When the whole is not sorted, find the part that is.** A rotation breaks order in exactly one place, so any split leaves at least one side untouched by the break. On that side a range check is reliable; on the other it is not — so ask the question only where it can be answered.

## Approach
```java
int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;
while (lo <= hi) {
    int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
    if (a[mid] == target) return mid;
    if (a[lo] <= a[mid]) {                                   // left half sorted
        if (a[lo] <= target && target < a[mid]) hi = mid - 1;
        else lo = mid + 1;
    } else {                                                 // right half sorted
        if (a[mid] < target && target <= a[hi]) lo = mid + 1;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
}
return -1;
```

## The `<=` in `a[lo] <= a[mid]`
When `lo == mid` (two elements left), the left "half" is a single element and is sorted. A strict `<` misclassifies it and searches the wrong side. Distinct values are what make this test sufficient; with duplicates, `a[lo] == a[mid]` says nothing and the worst case becomes O(n).

