## The one thing this teaches
**Split a two-sided constraint into two one-sided ones.** A single left-to-right pass can only react to the neighbour it has seen; a decreasing run like `5 4 3 2 1` needs information flowing from the right. Two passes each enforce one side exactly, and taking the maximum satisfies both without adding anything extra.

## Approach
```java
int[] c = new int[n];
Arrays.fill(c, 1);
for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) if (r[i] > r[i - 1]) c[i] = c[i - 1] + 1;
for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) if (r[i] > r[i + 1]) c[i] = Math.max(c[i], c[i + 1] + 1);
long total = 0;
for (int x : c) total += x;
```

## Why it is minimal
After the left pass, `c[i]` is the smallest value satisfying every left constraint along the increasing run ending at i; the right pass likewise for the right. Any valid assignment must be at least both, so the max is a lower bound — and it is achieved.

## Equal ratings
Neighbours with equal ratings impose nothing: `1 2 2` needs `1 2 1`, total 4.

