## The one thing this teaches
**Break a cycle by fixing one decision.** A circular constraint has no starting point for a left-to-right DP. But in any valid choice, house 0 or house n−1 is left out — so the answer is the better of two *linear* problems, each with one end removed.

## Approach
```java
if (n == 1) return a[0];
return Math.max(rob(a, 0, n - 2), rob(a, 1, n - 1));

long rob(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {
    long prev = 0, cur = 0;          // best up to i-2, i-1
    for (int i = lo; i <= hi; i++) {
        long next = Math.max(cur, prev + a[i]);
        prev = cur;
        cur = next;
    }
    return cur;
}
```

## The overlap is fine
Both subproblems may skip *both* ends; that choice is counted twice, which does not matter for a maximum. The single-house case needs handling because removing an end from a one-house circle leaves nothing.

