## The one thing this teaches
**Re-index the problem until you recognise it.** Over the array positions this looks new. Over the *values* it is House Robber: adjacent values exclude each other, and each value is worth everything it contains.

## Approach
```java
long[] points = new long[max + 1];
for (int x : a) points[x] += x;
long prev = 0, cur = 0;
for (int v = 0; v <= max; v++) {
    long next = Math.max(cur, prev + points[v]);
    prev = cur;
    cur = next;
}
return cur;
```

## Why taking all copies is safe
Once one `x` is taken, every `x − 1` and `x + 1` is deleted, so the remaining copies of `x` have no one left to hurt. Choosing a value is all-or-nothing, which is what lets a single number, `points[x]`, represent it.

When values are huge and sparse, sort the distinct values instead and treat two as adjacent only if they differ by exactly 1.

