## The one thing this teaches
**Counting DP splits on the last choice.** Every way to form `t[:j]` from `s[:i]` either ignores `s[i−1]` or uses it to match `t[j−1]`. The two sets are disjoint, so their counts add:

`ways[i][j] = ways[i−1][j] + (s[i−1] == t[j−1] ? ways[i−1][j−1] : 0)`

## Approach — one row
```java
long[] ways = new long[m + 1];
ways[0] = 1;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int j = m; j >= 1; j--)          // back to front!
        if (s.charAt(i) == t.charAt(j - 1)) ways[j] = (ways[j] + ways[j - 1]) % MOD;
return ways[m];
```

## Why backwards
The update needs the *previous row's* `ways[j − 1]`. Iterating `j` upward overwrites it first, and a single `s` letter gets used twice — `s = "a"`, `t = "aa"` would count 1 instead of 0. The same reason 0/1 knapsack iterates capacity downward.

## Why a modulus
The count grows exponentially — `s` of 1000 `a`s and `t` of 500 `a`s is C(1000, 500) — so even a `long` cannot hold it.

