## The one thing this teaches
**Sorting deletes constraints.** Three triangle inequalities become one when you know which side is largest, and sorted order tells you. Then fixing the largest side leaves a two-pointer counting problem.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(a);
long count = 0;
for (int k = n - 1; k >= 2; k--) {
    int l = 0, r = k - 1;
    while (l < r) {
        if (a[l] + a[r] > a[k]) { count += r - l; r--; }   // every l' in [l, r) pairs with r
        else l++;
    }
}
```

## Counting a whole range at once
When `a[l] + a[r] > a[k]`, increasing `l` only makes the sum bigger, so all `r − l` choices of the smaller side work with this `r`. Adding them in one step is what keeps each `k` linear.

## Zeros
A side of length 0 can never form a triangle: `0 + x > y` needs `x > y`, but `y` is the largest. The strict inequality handles it with no special case.

