## The one thing this teaches
**A recursive definition, computed iteratively.** `say(n) = describe(say(n − 1))` is naturally recursive, and the recursion is a straight chain — one call per level with no branching — so a loop computes it with no stack at all.

## Approach
```java
String cur = "1";
for (int k = 2; k <= n; k++) {
    StringBuilder next = new StringBuilder();
    for (int i = 0; i < cur.length(); ) {
        int j = i;
        while (j < cur.length() && cur.charAt(j) == cur.charAt(i)) j++;
        next.append(j - i).append(cur.charAt(i));
        i = j;
    }
    cur = next.toString();
}
```

## Run-length encoding again
`describe` is the run-length encoder from the strings unit with count and character swapped. Recognising an old tool inside a new definition is most of the problem.

## A curiosity
No term ever contains a digit above 3, and each term is about 30% longer than the one before (Conway's constant, ≈ 1.3036).

