## The one thing this teaches
**Close the ring, then cut it.** Rotation of an array is awkward in place; rotation of a *ring* is just choosing where the list starts. Linking the tail back to the head costs one assignment, and the rotation becomes "break the ring after the right node".

## Approach
```java
if (head == null) return null;
int n = 1;
ListNode tail = head;
while (tail.next != null) { tail = tail.next; n++; }
k %= n;
if (k == 0) return head;
tail.next = head;                                   // ring
ListNode newTail = head;
for (int i = 0; i < n - k - 1; i++) newTail = newTail.next;
ListNode newHead = newTail.next;
newTail.next = null;                                // cut
return newHead;
```

## The modulus first
Without `k %= n`, a k of 2·10⁹ walks the ring two billion times. And `n` must be computed before the modulus, so an empty list is handled before dividing by zero.

