## The one thing this teaches
**Stability comes free when you append.** Appending each node at the tail of its group preserves the original relative order, with no bookkeeping. Two dummy heads make both groups uniform, even when one stays empty.

## Approach
```java
ListNode smallHead = new ListNode(0, null), bigHead = new ListNode(0, null);
ListNode small = smallHead, big = bigHead;
for (ListNode p = head; p != null; p = p.next) {
    if (p.val < k) { small.next = p; small = p; }
    else          { big.next = p;   big = p; }
}
big.next = null;              // essential
small.next = bigHead.next;
return smallHead.next;
```

## The line that prevents a cycle
`big.next = null`. The last big node still points to whatever followed it in the original list — often a small node that is now earlier in the result. Leaving it creates a cycle, and printing the list never ends.

It is the stable-partition step of quicksort, on a list.

