## The one thing this teaches
**Merge sort is the list's sort.** Arrays favour quicksort for its cache behaviour; lists have no cache behaviour to protect and no random access for pivots, but splitting at the middle and merging two sorted lists are both linear and in-place. So merge sort gets O(n log n) with no extra array.

## Approach
```java
ListNode sort(ListNode head) {
    if (head == null || head.next == null) return head;
    ListNode slow = head, fast = head.next;          // fast starts one ahead
    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next; }
    ListNode right = slow.next;
    slow.next = null;                                // cut
    return merge(sort(head), sort(right));
}
```

## Why `fast = head.next`
With two nodes and `fast = head`, `slow` advances to the second node, `right` is null, and the left half is the whole list again — infinite recursion. Starting `fast` one ahead makes `slow` stop at the *first* middle, so both halves shrink.

