## The one thing this teaches
**Index the tree as if it were an array.** A complete binary tree stored in an array puts node p's children at 2p and 2p + 1. Giving every real node that index measures gaps without ever creating null nodes.

## Approach
```java
queue of (node, pos), starting (root, 0)
while (queue not empty) {
    size = queue.size(); first = pos of the first entry;
    for each entry in this level:
        last = pos;
        rel = pos - first;                       // normalise
        add (left, 2*rel), (right, 2*rel + 1)
    best = max(best, last - first + 1);
}
```

## Why normalise
On a 3000-node chain of right children, the raw position at the bottom is 2³⁰⁰⁰ — no integer type holds it. Subtracting the level's first position keeps every position below the level's width, which the statement promises fits in 32 bits, so the children fit in a `long`.

