## The one thing this teaches
**Tree DP returns more than one value.** A single "best for this subtree" is not enough to decide the parent, because the parent needs to know what the child's subtree is worth *without* the child. Returning both states makes each subtree self-describing.

## Approach
```java
long[] dfs(TreeNode t) {                 // {robbed, skipped}
    if (t == null) return new long[]{0, 0};
    long[] l = dfs(t.left), r = dfs(t.right);
    long robbed = t.val + l[1] + r[1];
    long skipped = Math.max(l[0], l[1]) + Math.max(r[0], r[1]);
    return new long[]{robbed, skipped};
}
answer = max(dfs(root))
```

## Why levels fail
In `4 → 1 → 2 → 3` (a chain), the best is 4 + 3 = 7: two nodes at depths 0 and 3, neither "all even" nor "all odd" levels.

The memoised version that recurses into grandchildren also works, but visits each node several times; the pair return visits each once.

