## The one thing this teaches
**Backtracking lives or dies by its pruning.** The raw search is 4¹⁵ ≈ 10⁹ assignments. Three cheap rules cut it to almost nothing: reject early on arithmetic, place the largest sticks first, and never try two sides that are currently the same length.

## Approach
```java
boolean place(int i) {
    if (i == n) return true;          // every stick placed, all sides ≤ target, total fits
    for (int s = 0; s < 4; s++) {
        if (side[s] + a[i] > target) continue;
        if (s > 0 && side[s] == side[s - 1]) continue;   // same state as the side before
        side[s] += a[i];
        if (place(i + 1)) return true;
        side[s] -= a[i];
    }
    return false;
}
```

## Why equal sides can be skipped
Putting the stick on either of two equally long sides leads to identical subproblems with the sides relabelled. Trying both doubles the work for nothing — and at the first stick all four sides are 0, so this alone divides the search by 24.

## Why descending
A long stick has few legal sides, so it fails in the first few levels instead of after the short sticks have filled the tree.

