## The one thing this teaches
**Store the answer in the structure.** A trie groups keys by prefix; if each node also stores the sum of the values in its subtree, `sum(prefix)` is a walk to one node and a read — no traversal of the subtree.

## Approach
```java
Map<String, Integer> value = new HashMap<>();
Node root = new Node();   // node.sum, node.next[26]

void insert(String key, int val) {
    int delta = val - value.getOrDefault(key, 0);
    value.put(key, val);
    Node cur = root;
    for (char c : key.toCharArray()) {
        if (cur.next[c - 'a'] == null) cur.next[c - 'a'] = new Node();
        cur = cur.next[c - 'a'];
        cur.sum += delta;
    }
}
int sum(String prefix) {
    Node cur = root;
    for (char c : prefix.toCharArray()) { cur = cur.next[c - 'a']; if (cur == null) return 0; }
    return cur.sum;
}
```

## The overwrite
Inserting `apple` with 3 and then 2 must leave 2, not 5. Adding the *difference* to every node on the path keeps all the stored sums correct, which is why the plain map of current values is needed alongside the trie.

