## The one thing this teaches
**Choose the state so that it composes.** "Largest square anywhere" does not combine from neighbours; "largest square ending exactly here" does, because a square at (i, j) is the union of three smaller squares at its up, left and diagonal neighbours plus the corner itself.

## Approach
```java
int[][] side = new int[r + 1][c + 1];     // padded, so row/column 0 are zeros
int best = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= r; i++)
    for (int j = 1; j <= c; j++)
        if (g[i - 1][j - 1] == '1') {
            side[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(side[i - 1][j - 1], Math.min(side[i - 1][j], side[i][j - 1]));
            best = Math.max(best, side[i][j]);
        }
return best * best;
```

## Why the minimum
The square can grow only as far as its weakest neighbour allows. If the cell above supports side 3 but the diagonal only 1, a side-3 square here would need a 1-square at the diagonal of side 2 — which does not exist.

## Area, not side
The answer is the *area*: square the side at the end.

