## The one thing this teaches
**Simulate only what the output needs.** The picture has columns and spaces, but reading row by row discards them. What survives is the order of characters within each row — and that is just "which row did this character land in".

## Approach
```java
if (rows == 1) return s;
StringBuilder[] row = new StringBuilder[rows];   // each initialised
int r = 0, step = 1;
for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
    row[r].append(c);
    if (r == 0) step = 1;
    else if (r == rows - 1) step = -1;
    r += step;
}
return String.join("", row);
```

## The closed form
The pattern repeats every `2·rows − 2` characters. Row 0 takes indices `0, cycle, 2·cycle…`; a middle row `i` takes `j·cycle ± i`. It is faster in theory and slower to get right.

## The one-row case
With one row, `r == 0` and `r == rows − 1` are both true and the step flips every character, sending `r` out of range. Return `s` first.

