## The one thing this teaches
**Multi-source BFS computes nearest-source distances for everyone at once.** Putting all sources in the queue at distance 0 is equivalent to adding a super-source connected to each of them; BFS layers then expand outward from all land simultaneously, and each cell is claimed by its closest land.

## Approach
```java
for (each land cell) q.add(cell);          // distance 0
if (q.isEmpty() || q.size() == r * c) return -1;
int dist = -1;
while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    dist++;
    for (int k = q.size(); k > 0; k--) {
        int[] cur = q.poll();
        for (each water neighbour) { mark it land; q.add(it); }
    }
}
return dist;
```

## Counting layers
The loop runs once per BFS layer; the first layer is the land itself (distance 0), so starting the counter at −1 makes the final value the distance of the farthest layer.

