## The one thing this teaches
**Some graph questions are about degrees, not paths.** "Trusted by everyone else and trusts no one" is a statement about in-degree and out-degree. No adjacency list is needed.

## Approach
```java
int[] score = new int[n + 1];
for (int[] t : trust) { score[t[0]]--; score[t[1]]++; }
for (int p = 1; p <= n; p++) if (score[p] == n - 1) return p;
return -1;
```

## Why one score is enough
The maximum in-degree is n − 1. A score of n − 1 therefore needs in-degree n − 1 *and* out-degree 0 — any trust given would pull it below. And at most one person can be trusted by all others while trusting none.

With `n = 1` and no pairs, the lone person is the judge: score 0 = n − 1.

