## The one thing this teaches
**Binary search on the answer, with a counting check.** "How many entries are ≤ v?" is monotone in v, and in a matrix sorted both ways it can be answered in O(n) by walking a staircase. The k-th smallest is the smallest v whose count reaches k.

## Approach
```java
long lo = m[0][0], hi = m[n - 1][n - 1];
while (lo < hi) {
    long mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;          // careful with negatives: use floor division
    if (countAtMost(mid) >= k) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
}
return lo;

long countAtMost(long v) {
    long count = 0;
    int i = n - 1, j = 0;
    while (i >= 0 && j < n) {
        if (m[i][j] <= v) { count += i + 1; j++; }   // whole column above is ≤ v
        else i--;
    }
    return count;
}
```

## Why the answer is in the matrix
The search finds the smallest v with count ≥ k. Any such minimal v must be a value that occurs — between two consecutive matrix values the count does not change.

## Negatives
`(lo + hi) / 2` rounds toward zero in Java; with negative bounds that can loop forever. `lo + (hi − lo) / 2` with `hi − lo ≥ 0` always rounds down.

