## The one thing this teaches
**Only store what does not follow a pattern.** The free seats are "every number above `next`" plus a scattered set of returned seats. The first part is one integer; only the scattered part needs a heap.

## Approach
```java
PriorityQueue<Integer> freed = new PriorityQueue<>();
int next = 1;

int reserve() {
    if (!freed.isEmpty()) return freed.poll();   // every freed seat is < next
    return next++;
}
void unreserve(int s) { freed.add(s); }
```

## Why the heap's top always wins
A seat can only be freed after being reserved, and every reserved seat is below `next`. So whenever the heap is non-empty, its smallest element is smaller than `next` — no comparison needed.

