## The one thing this teaches
**A sorted list is an implicit trie.** Every trie node corresponds to a contiguous block of the sorted words — the ones with that prefix. Binary search finds the start of the block; the first three entries are the three smallest.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(products);
String prefix = "";
for (char c : searchWord.toCharArray()) {
    prefix += c;
    int i = lowerBound(products, prefix);
    List<String> got = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int j = i; j < Math.min(i + 3, n) && products[j].startsWith(prefix); j++) got.add(products[j]);
    output(got);
}
```

## The trie version
Insert products in sorted order into a trie whose nodes keep a list of at most three words; the first three to pass through a node are its answer. More memory, and O(L) per keystroke with no search — the shape of real autocomplete.

