## The one thing this teaches
**Matching with multiplicity is a minimum of counts.** How many times can digit d be paired between two bags? As many as the smaller bag has. Summing that over the ten digits pairs everything that can pair, with no digit used twice.

## Approach
```java
int bulls = 0;
int[] s = new int[10], g = new int[10];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (secret.charAt(i) == guess.charAt(i)) bulls++;
    else { s[secret.charAt(i) - '0']++; g[guess.charAt(i) - '0']++; }
}
int cows = 0;
for (int d = 0; d < 10; d++) cows += Math.min(s[d], g[d]);
return bulls + "A" + cows + "B";
```

## Why exclude bulls from the counts
`secret = 1123`, `guess = 0111`: position 1 is a bull (the `1`). Counting it again as available for cows would give 2 cows; excluding bull positions leaves secret `{1,2,3}` and guess `{0,1,1}`, which share one `1` — 1A1B.

