## The one thing this teaches
**Canonical forms turn structure into keys.** Two subtrees are equal exactly when their serialisations are equal, so a hash map of serialisations finds every repeat — the same move as sorting letters to group anagrams.

## Approach
```java
Map<String, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();
int dups = 0;
String ser(TreeNode t) {
    if (t == null) return "#";
    String key = t.val + "," + ser(t.left) + "," + ser(t.right);
    if (seen.merge(key, 1, Integer::sum) == 2) dups++;      // count each shape once
    return key;
}
```

## The null markers matter
Without `#`, a node with only a left child and one with only a right child serialise identically. The markers make the string describe the shape.

## Going linear
Replace each serialisation with a small integer id — `(val, leftId, rightId)` mapped to an id — so keys have constant length.

starter_code.java:
