## The one thing this teaches
**Fix the target shape, then measure its cost.** The ones will occupy *some* window of length k. For a given window the cost is exactly its zero count — each zero inside is swapped with a one outside. So minimise zeros, i.e. maximise ones, over all windows of length k.

## Approach
```java
int k = count of ones;
if (k == 0) return 0;
int inWin = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) inWin += a[i];
int best = inWin;
for (int i = k; i < n + k; i++) {                 // wrap with % n
    inWin += a[i % n] - a[(i - k) % n];
    best = Math.max(best, inWin);
}
return k - best;
```

## The circle
Indexing `i % n` slides the window across the seam without copying the array twice. A linear version of the problem is the same loop stopped at `n`.

