## The one thing this teaches
**The pigeonhole principle as an algorithm.** The largest gap is at least the average gap, `(max − min) / (n − 1)`. Choose buckets narrower than that, and no gap *inside* a bucket can be the answer — so each bucket only needs its min and max, and the answer is found between neighbouring non-empty buckets.

## Approach
```java
int width = Math.max(1, (int) Math.ceil((double) (max - min) / (n - 1)));
int k = (max - min) / width + 1;
// bucketMin[k], bucketMax[k], seen[k]
for (int x : a) { int b = (x - min) / width; update min/max of bucket b; }
long prevMax = min, best = 0;
for (int b = 0; b < k; b++) if (seen[b]) {
    best = Math.max(best, bucketMin[b] - prevMax);
    prevMax = bucketMax[b];
}
```

## Why the width guard
If every value is equal, `max − min` is 0 and the width would be 0 — a division by zero. A width of at least 1 puts everything in one bucket and returns 0.

Radix sort also gives O(n) for bounded integers; buckets are the argument worth being able to make out loud.

