## The one thing this teaches
**Scan in the direction that fixes the hard part.** The pattern has three roles. Scanning right to left, the stack pairs each '3' with the best '2' to its right — the largest value smaller than it — so only the '1' remains to be found, and it is simply any value below that '2'.

## Approach
```java
long two = Long.MIN_VALUE;         // best '2' so far
Deque<Integer> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (a[i] < two) return "YES";   // a[i] is the '1'
    while (!st.isEmpty() && st.peek() < a[i]) two = st.pop();   // a[i] is a '3' for these
    st.push(a[i]);
}
return "NO";
```

## Why the last popped value is the best '2'
The stack is decreasing from bottom to top, so the values popped for a given `a[i]` increase; the last one is the largest value below `a[i]` among those to its right. A larger '2' gives the '1' the most room.

The O(n²) version — prefix minimum as the '1' and a scan for the '2' — is the honest first answer.

