## The one thing this teaches
**Start where one comparison eliminates something.** At the top-left, both neighbours are larger and a comparison tells you nothing about direction. At the top-right, left is smaller and down is larger, so every comparison rules out an entire row or column.

## Approach
```java
int i = 0, j = c - 1;
while (i < r && j >= 0) {
    if (m[i][j] == target) return "YES";
    if (m[i][j] > target) j--;        // the rest of column j is even bigger
    else i++;                         // the rest of row i is even smaller
}
return "NO";
```

## Why it is not a binary search
The matrix is not globally sorted — a row's last element can exceed the next row's first — so treating it as one sorted array (Search a 2D Matrix I) fails. The staircase uses exactly the order that is guaranteed.

