editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Kahn's algorithm on an undirected tree finds its centre.** Removing every leaf shortens every longest path by two — one from each end — without changing its middle. Repeating until one or two nodes remain leaves exactly the middle of the longest path, which is where the height is smallest.

## Approach
```java
if (n == 1) return [0];
int[] deg = new int[n];  // plus adjacency lists
Deque<Integer> leaves = all nodes with deg == 1;
int remaining = n;
while (remaining > 2) {
    remaining -= leaves.size();
    for (int k = leaves.size(); k > 0; k--) {
        int leaf = leaves.poll();
        for (int nb : adj.get(leaf)) if (--deg[nb] == 1) leaves.add(nb);
    }
}
return the nodes left in leaves, sorted;
```

## Why at most two
A path with an odd number of nodes has one middle node; an even one has two. Every tree's centre is one node or two adjacent nodes.

