editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**A ring buffer.** An array whose ends wrap around never shifts elements: inserting at either end just moves an index. `ArrayDeque` is exactly this, plus resizing.

## Approach
```java
int[] buf = new int[k];
int head = 0, size = 0;

boolean insertFront(int v) {
    if (size == k) return false;
    head = (head - 1 + k) % k;
    buf[head] = v;
    size++;
    return true;
}
boolean insertLast(int v) {
    if (size == k) return false;
    buf[(head + size) % k] = v;
    size++;
    return true;
}
boolean deleteFront() { if (size == 0) return false; head = (head + 1) % k; size--; return true; }
boolean deleteLast()  { if (size == 0) return false; size--; return true; }
int getRear() { return size == 0 ? -1 : buf[(head + size - 1) % k]; }
```

## Head and size, not head and tail
With separate head and tail indices, an empty buffer and a full one both have `head == tail`, and you need a spare slot or a flag to tell them apart. Storing the size removes the ambiguity.

