editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Divide and conquer on the last operation.** Enumerating parenthesisations directly is confusing; choosing which operator is applied *last* splits the problem into two independent smaller problems whose answers combine by a cross product.

## Approach
```java
List<Integer> ways(String e) {
    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < e.length(); i++) {
        char c = e.charAt(i);
        if (c != '+' && c != '-' && c != '*') continue;
        for (int l : ways(e.substring(0, i)))
            for (int r : ways(e.substring(i + 1)))
                res.add(c == '+' ? l + r : c == '-' ? l - r : l * r);
    }
    if (res.isEmpty()) res.add(Integer.parseInt(e));   // no operator: a number
    return res;
}
```

## Memoisation
The same substring is evaluated from many splits. Caching `ways` by substring turns repeated work into lookups — the step from recursion to interval DP.

## How many results
With k operators there are Catalan(k) parenthesisations: 1, 2, 5, 14, 42, … That growth is why the length is capped.

