## The one thing this teaches
**Count a range of pairs in one step.** After sorting, once `a[l] + a[r]` is below the target, so is `a[l]` plus anything between `l` and `r`. Adding `r − l` counts all of them without listing them — the move that turns O(n²) into O(n).

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(a);
long count = 0;
int l = 0, r = n - 1;
while (l < r) {
    if ((long) a[l] + a[r] < target) { count += r - l; l++; }
    else r--;
}
```

## Pricing it
The brute force is the complexity unit's lesson in miniature: n² pairs at n = 10⁵ is 5·10⁹ checks. Sorting first costs n log n and makes the count linear.

