## The one thing this teaches
**Return a pair to avoid a second pass.** The LCA depends on depths, and depths depend on the whole subtree. Returning `(depth, lca)` from each call lets every node decide from its children alone.

## Approach
```java
// returns {depth, lcaValue}
int[] dfs(TreeNode t) {
    if (t == null) return new int[]{0, -1};
    int[] l = dfs(t.left), r = dfs(t.right);
    if (l[0] == r[0]) return new int[]{l[0] + 1, t.val};   // deepest leaves on both sides
    return l[0] > r[0] ? new int[]{l[0] + 1, l[1]} : new int[]{r[0] + 1, r[1]};
}
```

## Why equal depths mean "here"
If the deepest leaves on the left and right are equally deep, they are all deepest leaves of this subtree, and they sit on both sides — so no lower node contains them all. A leaf returns its own value, since both null children have depth 0.

