## The one thing this teaches
**Two pointers work for 'closest' as well as 'equal'.** The moves are driven by the sign of `sum − target`, which is defined whether or not an exact match exists. What changes is only what you record: the best distance seen, not a hit.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(a);
long best = (long) a[0] + a[1] + a[2];
for (int i = 0; i < n - 2; i++) {
    int l = i + 1, r = n - 1;
    while (l < r) {
        long s = (long) a[i] + a[l] + a[r];
        if (Math.abs(s - target) < Math.abs(best - target)) best = s;
        if (s == target) return s;
        if (s < target) l++; else r--;
    }
}
```

## Why no sum is skipped that could win
When `s < target`, every sum with the same `i` and `l` and a smaller `r` is even further below the target, so discarding `l` loses nothing — the same argument as Two Sum II.

