## The one thing this teaches
**Index arithmetic assigns cells to groups.** Rows and columns are given; boxes need a formula. `(i / 3) * 3 + j / 3` numbers the boxes 0–8 in reading order, and then all 27 constraints are the same check: "have I seen this digit in this group?"

## Approach
```java
int[] row = new int[9], col = new int[9], box = new int[9];   // bitmasks
for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < 9; j++) {
        if (g[i][j] == '.') continue;
        int bit = 1 << (g[i][j] - '1'), b = (i / 3) * 3 + j / 3;
        if ((row[i] & bit) != 0 || (col[j] & bit) != 0 || (box[b] & bit) != 0) return "NO";
        row[i] |= bit; col[j] |= bit; box[b] |= bit;
    }
return "YES";
```

## Valid is not solvable
The question is only about repeats among the filled cells. A board can be valid and have no solution; checking solvability is the (much harder) Sudoku Solver.

