## The one thing this teaches
**Binary search a partition, not a value.** The median splits the combined data into two halves with every left element ≤ every right element. Choosing how many elements come from A fixes how many come from B, and whether the split is valid is monotone in that choice.

## Approach
```java
// ensure A is the shorter array; half = (n + m + 1) / 2
int lo = 0, hi = n;
while (lo <= hi) {
    int i = (lo + hi) / 2, j = half - i;
    long aL = i == 0 ? MIN : A[i - 1], aR = i == n ? MAX : A[i];
    long bL = j == 0 ? MIN : B[j - 1], bR = j == m ? MAX : B[j];
    if (aL <= bR && bL <= aR) {
        if ((n + m) % 2 == 1) return max(aL, bL);
        return (max(aL, bL) + min(aR, bR)) / 2.0;
    }
    if (aL > bR) hi = i - 1; else lo = i + 1;
}
```

## Why search the shorter array
Then `j = half − i` is always within B's bounds, and the search is logarithmic in the smaller size.

## The output format
The median of integers is an integer or ends in .5, so it can be printed exactly without floating-point formatting surprises: compute the sum of the two middle values and halve it by hand.

