## The one thing this teaches
**Reduce a 2-D problem to repeated 1-D ones.** Every rectangle has a bottom row. With the bottom row fixed, the column heights of consecutive 1s above it form a histogram, and Largest Rectangle in Histogram — already solved with a monotonic stack — gives the best rectangle ending there.

## Approach
```java
int[] h = new int[c];
int best = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < r; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < c; j++) h[j] = g[i][j] == '1' ? h[j] + 1 : 0;
    best = Math.max(best, largestInHistogram(h));
}
```

## Why heights reset to zero
A 0 in the current row breaks every rectangle through that column with this bottom row, however tall the column was above it.

This is why Largest Rectangle in Histogram is worth knowing cold: its main use is as a subroutine.

