## The one thing this teaches
**Pick the decision that makes subproblems independent.** If balloon k is burst first, the balloons on its left and right become neighbours, so the two sides depend on each other. If k is burst *last* in the open interval (l, r), everything left of k was burst while k stood as a wall — the two sides never meet.

## Approach
```java
int[] v = new int[n + 2];  v[0] = v[n + 1] = 1;  // copy nums into v[1..n]
long[][] dp = new long[n + 2][n + 2];
for (int len = 2; len <= n + 1; len++)
    for (int l = 0; l + len <= n + 1; l++) {
        int r = l + len;
        for (int k = l + 1; k < r; k++)
            dp[l][r] = Math.max(dp[l][r], dp[l][k] + dp[k][r] + (long) v[l] * v[k] * v[r]);
    }
return dp[0][n + 1];
```

## Why the boundaries are l and r
In the open interval (l, r), balloons l and r are not burst yet — they are outside it. So when k is the last one inside, its neighbours are exactly `v[l]` and `v[r]`. The padding 1s make the whole row an interval of the same kind.

