## The one thing this teaches
**A star is a choice between two smaller problems.** `x*` either contributes nothing — drop it and its letter from the pattern — or it consumes one character of `s` and stays available for more. Both options point to smaller prefixes, which is exactly what a DP table provides.

## Approach
```java
boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n + 1][m + 1];
dp[0][0] = true;
for (int j = 2; j <= m; j++) dp[0][j] = p.charAt(j - 1) == '*' && dp[0][j - 2];   // a*b*c* matches ""
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
    for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {
        char pc = p.charAt(j - 1);
        if (pc == '*') {
            boolean zero = dp[i][j - 2];
            boolean more = matches(s.charAt(i - 1), p.charAt(j - 2)) && dp[i - 1][j];
            dp[i][j] = zero || more;
        } else dp[i][j] = matches(s.charAt(i - 1), pc) && dp[i - 1][j - 1];
    }
return dp[n][m];
```

## Why `dp[i − 1][j]` for "one more"
The star stays at position j: having matched one character, the same `x*` may match again. Moving to `j − 2` there would allow only one copy.

## The first row
An empty string matches patterns like `a*b*`, because each starred element can be used zero times. Initialising `dp[0][j]` is where that is recorded.

