## The one thing this teaches
**Moving bits between two numbers.** Reading bits off the low end of one number and pushing them onto the low end of another reverses their order — the same trick as reversing the digits of an integer, in base 2.

## Approach
```java
long result = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {
    result = (result << 1) | (n & 1);
    n >>= 1;
}
```

## Exactly 32
Stopping when `n` reaches 0 loses the leading zeros, and those zeros must become trailing zeros of the result: reversing `1` gives `2³¹`, not `1`.

## Unsigned in Java
With `int`, the result's top bit is the sign bit, so values ≥ 2³¹ print as negatives. Keeping everything in a `long` — or using `Integer.toUnsignedString` — prints them correctly. `Integer.reverse` does the whole job in the library.

