## The one thing this teaches
**A root-to-node path is an array.** Subarray Sum Equals K works on any sequence, and the path from the root to the current node is one. The only addition is that the sequence changes as the DFS moves, so the prefix map must be undone when a node is left.

## Approach
```java
Map<Long, Integer> prefix = new HashMap<>();   // starts with {0: 1}
long dfs(TreeNode t, long run) {
    if (t == null) return 0;
    run += t.val;
    long count = prefix.getOrDefault(run - k, 0);
    prefix.merge(run, 1, Integer::sum);
    count += dfs(t.left, run) + dfs(t.right, run);
    prefix.merge(run, -1, Integer::sum);           // backtrack
    return count;
}
```

## Why backtrack
Without removing `run` on the way out, a prefix from one branch would pair with a node in a sibling branch — a "path" that goes up and back down, which the question forbids.

Values reach 10⁹ and paths are long: running sums need a `long`.

