## The one thing this teaches
**Enumerate by the centre, not by the ends.** Choosing both ends gives n² substrings to check. Choosing a centre gives 2n − 1 starting points, and growing outward checks each longer palindrome in O(1) — one new pair of characters.

## Approach
```java
int bestLo = 0, bestLen = 1;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int w = 0; w < 2; w++) {                 // w = 0: centre i; w = 1: gap after i
        int lo = i, hi = i + w;
        while (lo >= 0 && hi < n && s.charAt(lo) == s.charAt(hi)) { lo--; hi++; }
        int len = hi - lo - 1;
        if (len > bestLen) { bestLen = len; bestLo = lo + 1; }
    }
return s.substring(bestLo, bestLo + bestLen);
```

## Why strict `>` gives the leftmost
Two palindromes of equal length have the same parity, so they come from the same kind of centre — and for a fixed length, a later centre means a later start. The first one found is the leftmost; `>=` would keep the last.

## Beyond O(n²)
Manacher's algorithm reuses the mirror image of palindromes already found to reach O(n). It is rarely expected in an interview; the centre expansion is.

