## The one thing this teaches
**When the values index the array, the array is the hash table.** The answer lies in `1..n+1`, so only values in `1..n` matter, and each has a natural home: index `v − 1`.

## Approach
```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    while (a[i] >= 1 && a[i] <= n && a[a[i] - 1] != a[i]) {
        int j = a[i] - 1;
        int t = a[j]; a[j] = a[i]; a[i] = t;   // a[i] goes home
    }
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    if (a[i] != i + 1) return i + 1;
return n + 1;
```

## Why the nested loop is O(n)
Every swap places one value at its home, and a value at home is never moved again. At most n swaps happen across the whole run, however the `while` iterations are distributed.

## The duplicate trap
The condition is `a[a[i] − 1] != a[i]`, not `a[i] != i + 1`. With `2 2`, the second 2 is not at home but its home already holds a 2 — swapping them would loop forever.

