## The one thing this teaches
**A monotonic stack with a veto.** The usual rule — pop anything larger than the newcomer — produces the smallest sequence. Here a pop is vetoed when the popped letter will never appear again, because every letter must be kept once.

## Approach
```java
int[] last = new int[26];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) last[s.charAt(i) - 'a'] = i;
boolean[] kept = new boolean[26];
StringBuilder st = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    char c = s.charAt(i);
    if (kept[c - 'a']) continue;
    while (st.length() > 0) {
        char top = st.charAt(st.length() - 1);
        if (top <= c || last[top - 'a'] < i) break;   // smaller, or last chance: keep it
        kept[top - 'a'] = false;
        st.setLength(st.length() - 1);
    }
    st.append(c);
    kept[c - 'a'] = true;
}
```

## Why skip letters already kept
In `cbacdcbc`, when the second `c` arrives (index 5) the stack is `a c d`. The kept `c` survived every push after it, so it already sits in its best position; a second copy would break "exactly once". Later, the `b` at index 6 cannot pop `d` — index 4 was the last `d` — which is why the answer ends `…d b`.

## Walkthrough: bcabc
`b` → `b`; `c` → `bc`; `a` pops `c` (seen again) and `b` (seen again) → `a`; `b` → `ab`; `c` → `abc`.

