## The one thing this teaches
**Decide on the last choice, and let it look back.** The pass covering the final travel day was bought at most 1, 7 or 30 days earlier. Whatever came before that pass is the same problem on a shorter calendar.

## Approach
```java
boolean[] travel = new boolean[366];
for (int d : days) travel[d] = true;
int[] cost = new int[366];
for (int d = 1; d <= 365; d++) {
    if (!travel[d]) { cost[d] = cost[d - 1]; continue; }
    cost[d] = Math.min(cost[d - 1] + c[0],
              Math.min(cost[Math.max(0, d - 7)] + c[1], cost[Math.max(0, d - 30)] + c[2]));
}
return cost[365];
```

## Why `max(0, d − 7)`
A 7-day pass ending on day 5 would have started before day 1. Clamping to day 0 — cost 0 — means "this pass covers everything so far".

