## The one thing this teaches
**Interval DP decides at the ends.** For a substring `s[i..j]`, only the outermost characters are special: they either match each other, or one of them gets a newly inserted mirror.

## Approach
```java
int[][] dp = new int[n][n];              // length-1 substrings: 0
for (int len = 2; len <= n; len++)
    for (int i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
        int j = i + len - 1;
        if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)) dp[i][j] = len == 2 ? 0 : dp[i + 1][j - 1];
        else dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
    }
return dp[0][n - 1];
```

## The same answer, another way
Every character either pairs with an existing character or with an inserted one. The characters that pair among themselves form a palindromic subsequence, so the fewest insertions is `n − (longest palindromic subsequence)` — itself the LCS of `s` and its reverse.

## Walkthrough: mbadm
The longest palindromic subsequence is `mam` (or `mdm`), length 3, so 5 − 3 = 2 insertions: `mbdadbm`.

