## The one thing this teaches
**Counting is easier than selecting.** Picking the k-th smallest of n² values directly is expensive. Counting how many are ≤ d is one linear pass, and that count is monotone in d — which is all binary search needs.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(a);
long lo = 0, hi = a[n - 1] - a[0];
while (lo < hi) {
    long mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    long count = 0;
    for (int right = 0, left = 0; right < n; right++) {
        while (a[right] - a[left] > mid) left++;
        count += right - left;   // pairs (left..right−1, right)
    }
    if (count >= k) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
}
return lo;
```

## Why the answer is a real distance
The search finds the smallest `d` with at least k pairs ≤ d. If `d` were not an actual distance, `d − 1` would have the same count and would have been chosen instead.

## Why sorting is allowed
The multiset of pair distances does not depend on the order of the array, so sorting changes nothing about the answer.

