## The one thing this teaches
**When every choice costs the same, take the biggest.** Each chosen value costs 1 regardless of how many elements it removes, so any optimal set can swap a smaller count for a larger unused one without getting worse.

## Approach
```java
Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
for (int x : a) freq.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);
List<Integer> counts = new ArrayList<>(freq.values());
counts.sort(Collections.reverseOrder());
int removed = 0, chosen = 0;
for (int c : counts) {
    removed += c; chosen++;
    if (2 * removed >= n) break;
}
return chosen;
```

## The exchange argument
Suppose an optimal set skips the largest count `C` but uses some count `c ≤ C`. Swapping `c` for `C` keeps the set size and removes at least as many elements. Repeating the swap turns any optimal set into the greedy one.

