## The one thing this teaches
**Bijective numeration: digits 1..26 instead of 0..25.** Ordinary base 26 would need a zero digit. Column names skip it, so each step first subtracts 1 to map the digit range 1..26 onto 0..25, then proceeds as usual.

## Approach
```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
while (n > 0) {
    n--;                                  // 1..26 → 0..25
    sb.append((char) ('A' + n % 26));
    n /= 26;
}
return sb.reverse().toString();
```

## Walkthrough: 28
n = 28, decremented to 27: letter `'A' + 1 = B`, then n = 27 / 26 = 1. n = 1, decremented to 0: letter `A`, then n = 0. Reversed: `AB`.

## The classic bug
Without the `n--`, 26 gives remainder 0 — printed as `A` — and a quotient of 1 that prints another letter: `BA` instead of `Z`.

