## The one thing this teaches
**Count in blocks, then index.** Numbers of the same length contribute equally many digits, so the sequence splits into blocks you can skip with arithmetic instead of generating them.

## Approach
```java
long len = 1, count = 9, start = 1;
while (n > len * count) {
    n -= len * count;       // skip every len-digit number
    len++; count *= 10; start *= 10;
}
long number = start + (n - 1) / len;
return Long.toString(number).charAt((int) ((n - 1) % len)) - '0';
```

## Walkthrough: n = 1000
Skip 9 (one-digit): n = 991. Skip 180 (two-digit): n = 811. 811 ≤ 2700, so the digit is in a three-digit number: `100 + 810 / 3 = 370`, position `810 % 3 = 0` → `3`.

## Use long
At the ten-digit block, `len * count` is 10 × 9·10⁹ = 9·10¹⁰ — far past an int, even though n itself fits in one.

