## The one thing this teaches
**A topological order is a DP order.** DP needs subproblems solved before they are used. In a DAG, "the best path ending at v" depends only on its predecessors — and a topological order puts every predecessor first.

## Approach
```java
// indegree counts, then a queue of indegree-0 nodes
long[] best = new long[n];   // a path may start anywhere
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int u = queue.poll();
    for (int[] e : adj[u]) {
        best[e[0]] = Math.max(best[e[0]], best[u] + e[1]);
        if (--indeg[e[0]] == 0) queue.add(e[0]);
    }
}
return max(best);
```

## Why cycles break it
With a cycle of positive weight, a path could loop forever, and "simple path" makes the problem NP-hard. Without cycles, every path is automatically simple and the DP is exact.

## The same trick elsewhere
Negating the weights turns this into a shortest path, and the same one pass solves shortest paths in a DAG — even with negative edges, where Dijkstra would fail.

