## The one thing this teaches
**What you store decides what you can answer.** Subarray Sum Equals K stores a *count* per prefix sum to count subarrays. Here the question is the longest, so each prefix sum stores the *earliest* index it appeared — the farthest left a matching subarray could start.

## Approach
```java
Map<Long, Integer> first = new HashMap<>();
first.put(0L, -1);
long prefix = 0;
int best = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    prefix += a[i];
    Integer j = first.get(prefix - k);
    if (j != null) best = Math.max(best, i - j);
    first.putIfAbsent(prefix, i);   // keep the earliest
}
```

## Two details that matter
- `first.put(0L, -1)` lets a subarray start at index 0.
- `putIfAbsent`, not `put`: overwriting with a later index would shorten every future match.

