## The one thing this teaches
**"Ending here" states reset; "best so far" states don't.** The longest common *subsequence* carries its best forward across mismatches. A *substring* must be contiguous, so its state is "the run ending exactly here" — which drops to 0 on a mismatch — and the answer is the best of all of them.

## Approach
```java
int[] prev = new int[m + 1];
int best = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    int[] cur = new int[m + 1];
    for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++)
        if (s.charAt(i - 1) == t.charAt(j - 1)) { cur[j] = prev[j - 1] + 1; best = Math.max(best, cur[j]); }
    prev = cur;
}
return best;
```

## Compared with LCS
| | match | mismatch | answer |
|---|---|---|---|
| subsequence | `dp[i−1][j−1] + 1` | `max(dp[i−1][j], dp[i][j−1])` | `dp[n][m]` |
| substring | `dp[i−1][j−1] + 1` | `0` | max over all cells |

## Faster, for the curious
A suffix automaton or binary search with rolling hashes reaches roughly O((n + m) log) — well beyond what interviews ask.

