## The one thing this teaches
**Cancelling in groups preserves the heavy hitters.** Boyer–Moore for a majority cancels pairs of different values. For "more than n/3", cancel *triples* of distinct values: each cancellation removes three elements but at most one copy of any heavy value, so a value with more than n/3 copies cannot be wiped out.

## Approach
```java
int c1 = 0, c2 = 0; long v1 = 0, v2 = 1;       // distinct placeholders
for (int x : a) {
    if (x == v1) c1++;
    else if (x == v2) c2++;
    else if (c1 == 0) { v1 = x; c1 = 1; }
    else if (c2 == 0) { v2 = x; c2 = 1; }
    else { c1--; c2--; }                        // cancel a triple
}
// second pass: count v1 and v2, keep those above n / 3
```

## Why the second pass is required
The vote guarantees that a heavy value survives, not that a survivor is heavy. In `1 2 3`, the vote ends with candidates that each appear once — not more than ⌊3/3⌋ = 1.

## Order of the checks
Matching a candidate must come before claiming an empty slot; otherwise a value already held in slot 1 could also be placed in slot 2 while `c2 == 0`, and both slots would hold the same value.

