## The one thing this teaches
**Generate predecessors instead of testing pairs.** A word of length L has at most L predecessors, and they are cheap to construct. Looking them up in a hash map beats comparing every pair of words.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(words, Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
Map<String, Integer> best = new HashMap<>();
int answer = 0;
for (String w : words) {
    int b = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < w.length(); i++) {
        String pred = w.substring(0, i) + w.substring(i + 1);
        b = Math.max(b, best.getOrDefault(pred, 0) + 1);
    }
    best.put(w, b);
    answer = Math.max(answer, b);
}
```

## Why sorting by length is enough
A predecessor is exactly one letter shorter, so it is processed before the word that needs it. Words of equal length never depend on each other — any order among them works.

## Walkthrough (Example 1)
`a`, `b` → 1. `ba` → 2 (from either). `bca`, `bda` → 3 (from `ba`). `bdca` → 4 (from `bca` or `bda`).

