## The one thing this teaches
**Reverse to turn suffixes into prefixes.** Tries answer prefix questions. Reversing every word makes "is this a suffix of that" a prefix question — and in a trie of reversed words, the words that must be written out are exactly those ending at leaves.

## Approach (sorted reversals)
```java
String[] rev = reversed copies of the words;
Arrays.sort(rev);
int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < rev.length; i++)
    if (i + 1 == rev.length || !rev[i + 1].startsWith(rev[i]))   // not a prefix of a longer one
        total += rev[i].length() + 1;
```

## Why checking only the next word works
In sorted order, every string that starts with `x` comes immediately after `x`, contiguously. If any word extends `x`, the very next one does. Duplicates are handled too: an identical copy "starts with" `x`.

## Walkthrough: time me bell
Reversed and sorted: `em`, `emit`, `lleb`. `em` is a prefix of `emit` → free. `emit` → 5. `lleb` → 5. Total 10: `time#bell#`.

