## The one thing this teaches
**Inclusion–exclusion lifts prefix sums to two dimensions.** In one dimension a range is `P[r+1] − P[l]`. In two, a rectangle is the big corner block, minus the block above, minus the block to the left, plus the corner that was subtracted twice.

## Approach
```java
long[][] P = new long[r + 1][c + 1];
for (int i = 0; i < r; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < c; j++)
        P[i + 1][j + 1] = m[i][j] + P[i][j + 1] + P[i + 1][j] - P[i][j];

long query(int r1, int c1, int r2, int c2) {
    return P[r2 + 1][c2 + 1] - P[r1][c2 + 1] - P[r2 + 1][c1] + P[r1][c1];
}
```

## The picture
```
P[r2+1][c2+1]   =  A | B
                   --+--
                   C | Q     ← Q is the query
```
`P[r1][c2+1]` is `A + B`, `P[r2+1][c1]` is `A + C`, and `P[r1][c1]` is `A`. So `(A+B+C+Q) − (A+B) − (A+C) + A = Q`.

## The padding row
The extra zero row and column mean a query touching row 0 or column 0 needs no special case.

