## The one thing this teaches
**Order the input so the dictionary is built for free.** Excluding the word itself from its own dictionary is the fiddly part of this problem. Processing words shortest-first means the set, at that moment, contains precisely the allowed pieces.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(words, Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
Set<String> dict = new HashSet<>();
for (String w : words) {
    if (!dict.isEmpty() && canBreak(w, dict)) result.add(w);
    dict.add(w);
}

boolean canBreak(String w, Set<String> dict) {
    boolean[] ok = new boolean[w.length() + 1];
    ok[0] = true;
    for (int i = 1; i <= w.length(); i++)
        for (int j = 0; j < i && !ok[i]; j++)
            ok[i] = ok[j] && dict.contains(w.substring(j, i));
    return ok[w.length()];
}
```

## With a trie
Walking a trie from position `j` finds every dictionary word starting there in one pass, instead of looking up each substring separately — the same DP with a faster inner loop.

## Equal lengths
Two words of the same length can never be pieces of each other, so the order among equal lengths does not matter.

