## The one thing this teaches
**XOR is "where do these differ".** Any question comparing two bit patterns position by position starts with `x ^ y`; what remains is counting.

## Approach
```java
long v = x ^ y;
int count = 0;
while (v != 0) {
    v &= v - 1;      // clear the lowest set bit
    count++;
}
return count;
```

## Why v & (v − 1) clears one bit
Subtracting 1 turns the lowest 1 into 0 and every 0 below it into 1. AND-ing with the original keeps everything above that bit and zeroes the rest: `1011000 & 1010111 = 1010000`.

## In practice
`Long.bitCount(x ^ y)` does the same, usually with a single CPU instruction.

