## The one thing this teaches
**Cancel per bit, modulo the repetition count.** XOR is addition modulo 2 on each bit, which is why it cancels pairs. For triples, add each bit column modulo 3 instead.

## Approach
```java
int result = 0;
for (int b = 0; b < 32; b++) {
    int ones = 0;
    for (int x : a) ones += (x >> b) & 1;
    if (ones % 3 != 0) result |= 1 << b;
}
return result;
```

## Negative numbers
In two's complement a negative number has bit 31 set. Counting that bit like any other and setting `1 << 31` rebuilds the negative value exactly — the sign needs no special handling.

## The generalisation
If every value appears `k` times except one, use `% k`. The same column-sum idea also underlies the constant-time two-variable state machine (`ones`, `twos`) often shown for this problem.

