## The one thing this teaches
**A small set is an integer.** A subset of 26 letters is a 26-bit number; union is `|`, intersection is `&`, and "disjoint" is `== 0`. The per-pair cost drops from O(L) to one machine instruction.

## Approach
```java
int[] mask = new int[n];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (char ch : words[i].toCharArray()) mask[i] |= 1 << (ch - 'a');
long best = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
        if ((mask[i] & mask[j]) == 0)
            best = Math.max(best, (long) words[i].length() * words[j].length());
```

## Masks forget counts
`foo` and `fo` have the same mask. That is exactly right here — sharing letters is about which letters, not how many — but it means a mask cannot answer questions like "is this an anagram".

## A small speedup
Words with the same mask can keep only the longest: at most 2^26 masks, and in practice far fewer than n.

