## The one thing this teaches
**Skip whole blocks of a recursion without entering them.** Generating permutations recursively fixes the first digit and recurses on the rest. Each first digit owns a block of exactly `(n − 1)!` permutations, so division tells you which block holds the k-th — recursion by arithmetic.

## Approach
```java
List<Integer> digits = [1..n];
int[] fact = factorials 0!..(n−1)!;
k--;                                     // zero-indexed
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = n; i >= 1; i--) {
    int block = fact[i - 1];
    sb.append(digits.remove(k / block));  // the (k / block)-th remaining digit
    k %= block;
}
```

## Walkthrough: n = 4, k = 9
k − 1 = 8. Blocks of 3! = 6: index 1 → digit `2`, k = 2. Blocks of 2! = 2 over `1 3 4`: index 1 → `3`, k = 0. Blocks of 1 over `1 4`: index 0 → `1`. Then `4`. Answer `2314`.

## Why k − 1
With zero-indexing, the digit indices are exactly the digits of k − 1 in the factorial number system. One-indexed k would put the last permutation of each block into the next block.

