## The one thing this teaches
**A monotonic stack can replay a traversal.** The stack holds the path of ancestors whose right subtrees have not started. A larger value closes those subtrees by popping them — and the closed nodes set a floor that nothing later may go under.

## Approach
```java
Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
long low = Long.MIN_VALUE;
for (int x : a) {
    if (x < low) return false;                      // below a node we already passed on the right
    while (!stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek() < x) low = stack.pop();
    stack.push(x);
}
return true;
```

## Walkthrough: 5 2 6 1 3
Push 5, push 2. The 6 pops 2 and 5: low = 5. The 1 is below 5 — but it came after the right turn at 5, so it cannot be in the tree. `NO`.

## The O(n) space question
The stack can be simulated inside the input array itself, reusing a prefix as the stack — O(1) extra space if modifying the input is allowed.

