## The one thing this teaches
**Choose the root, and independent sides multiply.** Once the root is fixed, the left and right subtrees are separate problems whose shapes combine freely — so their counts multiply, and summing over roots gives the total.

## Approach
```java
long[] G = new long[n + 1];
G[0] = 1;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
    for (int r = 1; r <= i; r++)
        G[i] += G[r - 1] * G[i - r];      // left has r − 1 values, right has i − r
return G[n];
```

## Why only the size matters
The values 4, 5, 6 can form exactly as many BSTs as 1, 2, 3 — relabelling preserves order. That is what lets one table serve every subrange.

