## The one thing this teaches
**Return a summary, not a yes/no.** A child saying "I am a BST" is not enough for its parent, which also needs the range of values below. Returning `(isBST, min, max, size)` lets every node decide in constant time.

## Approach
```java
// returns {isBST (1/0), min, max, size}
int[] visit(TreeNode t) {
    if (t == null) return new int[]{1, Integer.MAX_VALUE, Integer.MIN_VALUE, 0};
    int[] L = visit(t.left), R = visit(t.right);
    if (L[0] == 1 && R[0] == 1 && L[2] < t.val && t.val < R[1]) {
        int size = L[3] + R[3] + 1;
        best = Math.max(best, size);
        return new int[]{1, Math.min(L[1], t.val), Math.max(R[2], t.val), size};
    }
    return new int[]{0, 0, 0, 0};
}
```

## The empty-subtree sentinel
An empty child reports min = +∞ and max = −∞, so the comparisons `L.max < val < R.min` pass automatically — no null checks in the parent.

## Why checking children alone is wrong
In `10 5 15 1 12`, every node is ordered correctly against its own children — yet 12 sits in 10's left subtree. Comparing 10 with the left subtree's *maximum* (12), not its child (5), catches it. The largest BST is `5 → 1, 12`, with 3 nodes.

