## The one thing this teaches
**Keep only the candidates, in arrival order.** A queue of candidates is the gentle version of the monotonic deque: here no candidate ever makes another useless, so nothing is removed from the back — only expired ones from the front.

## Approach
```java
Deque<Integer> neg = new ArrayDeque<>();
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (a[i] < 0) neg.addLast(i);
    if (!neg.isEmpty() && neg.peekFirst() <= i - k) neg.pollFirst();   // left the window
    if (i >= k - 1) out.add(neg.isEmpty() ? 0 : a[neg.peekFirst()]);
}
```

## Why one removal per step is enough
The window slides by one, so at most one index leaves it each step — and only the oldest queued index can be that one.

## Compared with the sliding maximum
For the maximum, a new large value makes older smaller ones useless, so they are popped from the back. "First negative" has no such dominance: an older negative always wins while it is inside the window.

