## The one thing this teaches
**Peel digits with `% 10` and `/ 10`, and guard the divisor.** Digit loops are the bread and butter of number problems; the one trap here is a zero digit, which must be rejected before it is used in `n % d`.

## Approach
```java
boolean selfDividing(int n) {
    for (int v = n; v > 0; v /= 10) {
        int d = v % 10;
        if (d == 0 || n % d != 0) return false;   // order matters: d == 0 first
    }
    return true;
}
```

## Why `d == 0` must come first
`||` stops at the first true operand. With `d == 0` checked first, `n % d` is never evaluated for a zero digit — swap them and the program throws `ArithmeticException` on 10.

