## The one thing this teaches
**Precompute what every check shares.** The naive test rescans a row and a column per cell. Those scans repeat the same work r · c times; storing one minimum per row and one maximum per column removes the repetition.

## Approach
```java
int[] rowMin = new int[r], colMax = new int[c];
Arrays.fill(rowMin, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
for (int i = 0; i < r; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < c; j++) {
        rowMin[i] = Math.min(rowMin[i], m[i][j]);
        colMax[j] = Math.max(colMax[j], m[i][j]);
    }
// lucky: m[i][j] == rowMin[i] && m[i][j] == colMax[j]
```

## At most one
With distinct values there is never more than one lucky number. If `a` (row i, column j) and `b` (row k, column l) were both lucky, `a ≤ m[i][l] ≤ b` and `b ≤ m[k][j] ≤ a`, so `a = b`.

