## The one thing this teaches
**Count pairs by their second element.** Every pair is discovered exactly once — at its later index — by asking how many matching partners came before. That turns an O(n²) enumeration into one pass.

## Approach
```java
Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();
long pairs = 0;
for (int x : a) {
    int c = seen.getOrDefault(x, 0);
    pairs += c;            // x pairs with each earlier copy
    seen.put(x, c + 1);
}
```

## The closed form
A value appearing `c` times contributes `c(c − 1)/2` pairs — the same total, computed after counting. The running version is the same sum `0 + 1 + … + (c − 1)`, added one term at a time.

## Why this is a complexity lesson
The answer can be 5·10^9, far more than the 10^5 steps the algorithm takes. Counting pairs does not require visiting them.

