## The one thing this teaches
**Independent choices multiply; huge exponents need squaring.** The counting is one line — the multiplication principle. The engineering is raising to a power of 10^15 without 10^15 multiplications.

## Approach
```java
static final long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;

long power(long base, long e) {
    long result = 1;
    base %= MOD;
    while (e > 0) {
        if ((e & 1) == 1) result = result * base % MOD;
        base = base * base % MOD;   // base^(2^k)
        e >>= 1;
    }
    return result;
}

return power(5, (n + 1) / 2) * power(4, n / 2) % MOD;
```

## Why the products stay in range
Both factors are below 10^9 + 7, so their product is below about 1.0·10^18 — inside a signed 64-bit long (up to 9.2·10^18). Reduce after every multiplication.

