## The one thing this teaches
**A deterministic process with finite state must cycle — detect it with a map from state to time.** In long division the only state is the remainder, which is below the denominator. Seeing a remainder twice means the digits between are the period.

## Approach
```java
if (num == 0) return "0";
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
if ((num < 0) ^ (den < 0)) sb.append('-');
long n = Math.abs((long) num), d = Math.abs((long) den);
sb.append(n / d);
long rem = n % d;
if (rem == 0) return sb.toString();
sb.append('.');
Map<Long, Integer> at = new HashMap<>();
while (rem != 0) {
    if (at.containsKey(rem)) { sb.insert(at.get(rem), '('); sb.append(')'); break; }
    at.put(rem, sb.length());
    rem *= 10;
    sb.append(rem / d);
    rem %= d;
}
```

## Three traps
- **Sign**: `-50 / 8` and `50 / -8` are both negative, `0 / -5` is just `0`.
- **Overflow**: `Math.abs(-2147483648)` is still negative as an int. Convert to long first.
- **Where the cycle starts**: `1/6 = 0.1(6)`, not `0.(16)`. The map records *where* each remainder was first seen, so the parenthesis goes exactly there.

