## The one thing this teaches
**Event simulation: a sorted stream of arrivals feeding a priority queue of ready work.** The same shape schedules processes, orders deliveries and merges timelines — sort by when things appear, heap by which to handle first.

## Approach
```java
Integer[] order = indices sorted by arrive;
PriorityQueue<Integer> ready = new PriorityQueue<>((x, y) ->
    t[x][1] != t[y][1] ? Integer.compare(t[x][1], t[y][1]) : Integer.compare(x, y));
long time = 0;
int next = 0;
while (result.size() < n) {
    while (next < n && t[order[next]][0] <= time) ready.add(order[next++]);
    if (ready.isEmpty()) { time = t[order[next]][0]; continue; }   // idle: jump ahead
    int task = ready.poll();
    time += t[task][1];
    result.add(task);
}
```

## Why jump instead of ticking
Times reach 10^9. Advancing the clock one unit at a time while idle would take that many steps; jumping to the next arrival costs O(1).

## Use long for the clock
10^5 tasks of duration 10^9 finish at 10^14.

