## The one thing this teaches
**Enumerate with backtracking: extend, recurse, undo.** Listing all paths is a search tree whose leaves are the answers. In a DAG no node can repeat on a path, so the usual visited set is unnecessary — and would even be wrong, since other paths may pass through the same node.

## Approach
```java
void dfs(int u, List<Integer> path) {
    if (u == n - 1) { output(path); return; }
    for (int v : adj[u]) {          // sorted ascending
        path.add(v);
        dfs(v, path);
        path.remove(path.size() - 1);   // undo
    }
}
path = [0]; dfs(0, path);
```

## Why sorted adjacency gives sorted output
Two paths first differ at some position; the DFS reaches that branching node once and tries the smaller neighbour first, so every path through it is printed earlier.

## Compare numbers, not strings
As strings, `0 10 11` sorts before `0 2 11`. As integer sequences, 2 < 10, so `0 2 11` comes first.

