## The one thing this teaches
**Remove what disqualifies, then count what remains.** Checking "does this island touch the border" during each fill works, but a cleaner shape is two phases: sink every border-connected island, then count islands normally.

## Approach
```java
for (every border cell (i, j)) if (g[i][j] == 'L') sink(i, j);   // phase 1
int closed = 0;
for (every cell (i, j)) if (g[i][j] == 'L') { sink(i, j); closed++; }   // phase 2

void sink(int i, int j) {  // iterative flood fill turning L into W
    stack.push(i, j); g[i][j] = 'W';
    while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
        pop (x, y);
        for each 4-neighbour (a, b) inside with g[a][b] == 'L': g[a][b] = 'W'; push (a, b);
    }
}
```

## The same trick elsewhere
Surrounded Regions and Number of Enclaves use exactly this border-first pass. Anything that must *not* reach the edge is easiest to find by starting from the edge.

## Diagonals do not connect
Two land cells touching only at a corner are separate islands.

