## The one thing this teaches
**Backtracking as a binary decision tree.** Each letter is a yes/no choice, so the outputs are the leaves of a tree of depth n. The recursion is the tree; the order you try children in is the order of the output.

## Approach
```java
void build(char[] cur, int i) {
    if (i == cur.length) { output(new String(cur)); return; }
    if (Character.isDigit(cur[i])) { build(cur, i + 1); return; }
    cur[i] = Character.toUpperCase(cur[i]); build(cur, i + 1);
    cur[i] = Character.toLowerCase(cur[i]); build(cur, i + 1);
}
```

## Why no undo is needed
Position `i` is overwritten on every visit before anything reads it, so the array never carries a stale choice forward.

## The iterative view
Start with `[""]`. For each character, extend every string so far with it — or, for a letter, with both of its cases. The list doubles at each letter, the same tree built breadth-first.

