## The one thing this teaches
**Two heaps for two different orderings of the same things.** Free rooms are chosen by number; busy rooms are released by end time. One heap cannot serve both orders, so rooms move between two heaps as their state changes.

## Approach
```java
sort meetings by start;
PriorityQueue<Integer> free = rooms 0..k−1;
PriorityQueue<long[]> busy = by (end, room);
for (meeting (s, e)) {
    while (!busy.isEmpty() && busy.peek()[0] <= s) free.add((int) busy.poll()[1]);
    if (!free.isEmpty()) {
        int room = free.poll();
        busy.add(new long[]{e, room});  count[room]++;
    } else {
        long[] first = busy.poll();                  // earliest to finish
        busy.add(new long[]{first[0] + (e - s), first[1]});  count[(int) first[1]]++;
    }
}
```

## Why release before choosing
A room whose meeting ends exactly at `s` is free at `s` — intervals are half-open. Releasing every room with `end ≤ s` first lets the "lowest free room" rule see all of them, not just the one that frees earliest.

## Delays compound
A delayed meeting ends later, which can delay the next one further. With 10^5 meetings the end times can climb far past the input values.

