## The one thing this teaches
**k-Sum is (k − 2) loops around Two Sum.** 3Sum fixes one value; 4Sum fixes two. The duplicate-skipping rule is the same at every level: after handling a value at some position, skip the equal values that follow it.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(a);
long count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (i > 0 && a[i] == a[i - 1]) continue;
    for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
        if (j > i + 1 && a[j] == a[j - 1]) continue;
        int lo = j + 1, hi = n - 1;
        while (lo < hi) {
            long sum = (long) a[i] + a[j] + a[lo] + a[hi];
            if (sum < target) lo++;
            else if (sum > target) hi--;
            else {
                count++;
                do lo++; while (lo < hi && a[lo] == a[lo - 1]);
                do hi--; while (lo < hi && a[hi] == a[hi + 1]);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Why `j > i + 1` in the skip
The first `j` after `i` may equal `a[i]` legitimately — `[2, 2, 2, 2]` uses the same value four times. Only a *repeat at the same position* is a duplicate.

## Overflow
`4 × 10^9` does not fit in an int. Cast before adding, not after.

