editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**One DP value can answer two questions.** The largest square ending at a cell also tells you how many squares end there: sides 1, 2, …, s. So the Maximal Square table, summed instead of maximised, counts every square once.

## Approach
```java
long total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < r; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < c; j++) {
        if (m[i][j] == 1 && i > 0 && j > 0)
            m[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(m[i - 1][j - 1], Math.min(m[i - 1][j], m[i][j - 1]));
        total += m[i][j];
    }
```

## Why the minimum of three
A square of side s ending at (i, j) contains a square of side s − 1 ending at each of the three neighbours. The smallest of them limits how far the square can grow.

## Counted exactly once
Every square has one bottom-right corner, and at that corner it is counted in exactly one of the sizes 1..dp.

