editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Maximise a minimum by searching the answer.** "Make the smallest gap as large as possible" is hard directly, but "can every gap be at least d?" is a greedy check — and its answer flips from yes to no exactly once as d grows.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(pos);
long lo = 1, hi = pos[n - 1] - pos[0];
while (lo < hi) {
    long mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2;            // upper middle: we search for the LAST yes
    if (fits(pos, k, mid)) lo = mid; else hi = mid - 1;
}
return lo;

boolean fits(int[] pos, int k, long d) {
    int placed = 1;
    long last = pos[0];
    for (int p : pos) if (p - last >= d) { placed++; last = p; }
    return placed >= k;
}
```

## Why the greedy check is right
Any valid placement can be shifted so its first ball is in the leftmost basket, and each later ball moved left to the earliest basket that keeps the gap — never making a later placement harder.

## Upper middle
When `lo = mid` on success, rounding `mid` down could leave `lo` unchanged forever. `(lo + hi + 1) / 2` guarantees progress.

