## The one thing this teaches
**A bijection is two functions.** Checking one direction lets `a → dog` and `b → dog` through. Both directions must be consistent, so both need a map.

## Approach
```java
if (pattern.length() != words.length) return false;
Map<Character, String> toWord = new HashMap<>();
Map<String, Character> toLetter = new HashMap<>();
for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    char p = pattern.charAt(i);
    String w = words[i];
    if (toWord.containsKey(p) && !toWord.get(p).equals(w)) return false;
    if (toLetter.containsKey(w) && toLetter.get(w) != p) return false;
    toWord.put(p, w);
    toLetter.put(w, p);
}
return true;
```

## The counting shortcut
The mapping is a bijection exactly when `|distinct letters| = |distinct words| = |distinct (letter, word) pairs|`. It is one line in Python and a nice check on the map version.

## Compare strings with equals
In Java, `==` on strings compares references. Words read separately are different objects even when equal.

