editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Distribute the rules over the leaves.** Doubling distributes over addition: `((A)(B)) = 2(A + B) = 2A + 2B`. Pushing every doubling down to the innermost `()` shows each is worth `2^depth`, and the total is their sum.

## Approach
```java
long score = 0;
int depth = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
    if (s.charAt(i) == '(') depth++;
    else {
        depth--;
        if (s.charAt(i - 1) == '(') score += 1L << depth;   // a "()" at this depth
    }
}
```

## The stack version
```java
Deque<Long> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
st.push(0L);
for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
    if (ch == '(') st.push(0L);
    else { long v = st.pop(); st.push(st.pop() + Math.max(2 * v, 1)); }
}
return st.pop();
```
It mirrors the grammar directly and generalises to other scoring rules.

## Walkthrough: (()(()))
The first `()` is at depth 1 (worth 2); the second at depth 2 (worth 4). Total 6.

