## The one thing this teaches
**Monotone timestamps turn a priority queue into a queue.** In general, expiring the soonest item needs a heap. When every new expiry is the latest yet, insertion order *is* expiry order — and an insertion-ordered map both looks up by ID and expires from the front.

## Approach
```java
LinkedHashMap<String, Integer> expiry = new LinkedHashMap<>();

void expire(int t) {
    Iterator<Map.Entry<String, Integer>> it = expiry.entrySet().iterator();
    while (it.hasNext() && it.next().getValue() <= t) it.remove();
}

void generate(String id, int t) { expire(t); expiry.put(id, t + ttl); }
void renew(String id, int t) {
    expire(t);
    if (expiry.containsKey(id)) { expiry.remove(id); expiry.put(id, t + ttl); }   // move to the back
}
int count(int t) { expire(t); return expiry.size(); }
```

## Why remove then put
`LinkedHashMap.put` on an existing key keeps its old position. Removing first makes the renewed token the newest entry, preserving the order.

## The boundary
"Expires at t" means gone at t, so the test is `expiry <= t`.

