## The one thing this teaches
**Reason about repetition through what one period does.** A repeated process is determined by its effect per cycle. Here that effect is a shift and a rotation, and rotations have order at most 4.

## Approach
```java
int[][] dir = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {-1, 0}};   // N, E, S, W
int x = 0, y = 0, d = 0;
for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
    if (ch == 'G') { x += dir[d][0]; y += dir[d][1]; }
    else if (ch == 'R') d = (d + 1) % 4;
    else d = (d + 3) % 4;        // left = three rights, avoiding negative %
}
return (x == 0 && y == 0) || d != 0;
```

## Why four passes return
If one pass ends facing east (a 90° turn), the next pass's displacement is the first one rotated by 90°, and so on. Four displacements rotated by 0°, 90°, 180° and 270° sum to zero. A 180° turn returns after two passes.

## Direction as an index
Storing the direction as 0–3 into a vector table turns turning into modular arithmetic and moving into one addition.

