## The one thing this teaches
**Arrays with jump rules are graphs.** No edge list is stored — the neighbours of `i` are computed from `a[i]` — but reachability is plain BFS, and the visited array is what makes it terminate.

## Approach
```java
boolean[] seen = new boolean[n];
Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
q.add(start); seen[start] = true;
while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    int i = q.poll();
    if (a[i] == 0) return true;
    for (int j : new int[]{i + a[i], i - a[i]})
        if (j >= 0 && j < n && !seen[j]) { seen[j] = true; q.add(j); }
}
return false;
```

## Cycles are common
In `1 1`, index 0 jumps to 1 and index 1 jumps back to 0. Marking on enqueue guarantees each index is processed once.

## BFS or DFS?
Only reachability matters, not the number of jumps, so either works. An iterative version avoids deep recursion on long jump chains.

