## The one thing this teaches
**Minimum swaps = elements − cycles.** Whenever swaps untangle a structure made of cycles, each swap can split at most one cycle into two. A cycle of size c starts as one component and must end as c, so it needs c − 1 swaps — and the greedy "fix one couch at a time" achieves exactly that.

## Approach
```java
int[] parent = couples 0..m−1;
int components = m;
for (int couch = 0; couch < m; couch++) {
    int a = row[2 * couch] / 2, b = row[2 * couch + 1] / 2;   // couples on this couch
    if (union(a, b)) components--;
}
return m - components;
```

## The greedy view
Walk the couches left to right. If a couch's second person is not the first person's partner, swap the partner into that seat. Each swap completes one couch and never breaks a finished one — and it produces the same count.

## Walkthrough: 0 2 1 3
Couch 0 holds couples 0 and 1; couch 1 holds couples 0 and 1. One component of size 2: 2 − 1 = 1 swap.

