editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Offline queries: reorder them so the data only grows.** A trie supports inserts cheaply but not "ignore values above m". Sorting the queries by m turns the filter into a sequence of inserts, and the original order is restored at the end.

## Approach
```java
sort a ascending;
sort query indices by m;
int next = 0;
for (int qi : sortedQueries) {
    while (next < n && a[next] <= m[qi]) insert(a[next++]);
    answer[qi] = next == 0 ? -1 : bestXor(x[qi]);
}

int bestXor(int x) {
    Node node = root; int result = 0;
    for (int b = 29; b >= 0; b--) {
        int want = ((x >> b) & 1) ^ 1;          // the opposite bit makes this bit 1
        if (node.child[want] != null) { result |= 1 << b; node = node.child[want]; }
        else node = node.child[want ^ 1];
    }
    return result;
}
```

## Why greedy from the top bit
A 1 in bit b outweighs every lower bit combined (2^b > 2^b − 1). So whenever the opposite bit exists, taking it is always right.

## 30 bits
Values up to 10^9 < 2^30 fit in bits 29..0.

