## The one thing this teaches
**DP over "where am I now".** Counting sequences becomes easy when the future depends only on the current position — here, the last digit. The whole history collapses into ten numbers.

## Approach
```java
int[][] moves = {{4, 6}, {6, 8}, {7, 9}, {4, 8}, {0, 3, 9}, {}, {0, 1, 7}, {2, 6}, {1, 3}, {2, 4}};
long[] count = new long[10];
Arrays.fill(count, 1);
for (int step = 1; step < n; step++) {
    long[] next = new long[10];
    for (int d = 0; d < 10; d++)
        for (int to : moves[d]) next[to] = (next[to] + count[d]) % MOD;
    count = next;
}
return sum(count) % MOD;
```

## Why moves are symmetric
A knight move reversed is a knight move, so "jump from d to e" and "jump from e to d" are the same list. Either direction of the update works.

## The 5 key
No knight move reaches or leaves 5. It counts only for n = 1.

