## The one thing this teaches
**When pieces leave ragged edges, add a state for the ragged edge.** A flat boundary is not the only shape a partial tiling can end with. Naming the other shape — one cell protruding — makes the transitions local again.

## Approach
```java
long[] full = new long[n + 1], part = new long[n + 1];
full[0] = 1; full[1] = 1; part[1] = 0;
for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    full[i] = (full[i - 1] + full[i - 2] + 2 * part[i - 1]) % MOD;
    part[i] = (part[i - 1] + full[i - 2]) % MOD;
}
return full[n];
```

## The transitions
- **full[i]**: end with a vertical domino (from full[i−1]), two horizontal dominoes (from full[i−2]), or a tromino closing a protrusion (from part[i−1], top or bottom — hence the 2).
- **part[i]** (one cell of column i filled): a tromino on a flat edge (from full[i−2]), or a horizontal domino extending a protrusion (from part[i−1]).

## Walkthrough: n = 3
full[2] = 2 and part[2] = part[1] + full[0] = 1, so full[3] = full[2] + full[1] + 2·part[2] = 2 + 1 + 2 = 5.

