## The one thing this teaches
**Reverse a process when the reverse choices are forced.** Forwards, doubling early multiplies every later subtraction's effect — a hard trade-off. Backwards, halving is only possible on even numbers, and when it is possible it is always at least as good.

## Approach
```java
long ops = 0;
while (target > start) {
    target = target % 2 == 0 ? target / 2 : target + 1;
    ops++;
}
return ops + (start - target);
```

## Why halving first is right
Two +1 steps then a halve reach `(t + 2) / 2 = t/2 + 1`. A halve then one +1 reaches the same number in two steps instead of three. Delaying a halve never helps.

## Walkthrough: 3 → 10
Backwards: 10 → 5 (halve) → 6 (+1) → 3 (halve). Three operations: forwards 3 → 6 → 5 → 10.

