## The one thing this teaches
**Store direction as an edge attribute and traverse undirected.** Directed edges hide half the tree from a directed search. Adding every road in both directions, tagged with whether it is original, lets one traversal see everything and judge each road.

## Approach
```java
// adj[u] holds {v, 1} for an original road u → v and {v, 0} for the reverse view
boolean[] seen = new boolean[n];
Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(0));
seen[0] = true;
int flips = 0;
while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
    int u = stack.pop();
    for (int[] e : adj[u]) {
        if (seen[e[0]]) continue;
        seen[e[0]] = true;
        flips += e[1];               // original road points away from 0
        stack.push(e[0]);
    }
}
```

## Why no choices exist
Each road lies on the unique path between its far endpoint and 0, so its correct direction is fixed. The answer is simply the number of roads pointing the wrong way.

## Depth view
Equivalently, with depths from 0 in the undirected tree, a road `u → v` needs reversing exactly when `v` is deeper than `u`.

