editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Grid DP with a wider neighbourhood is still grid DP.** Minimum Path Sum looks at two predecessors; here there are three. The structure — each row depends only on the one before — is unchanged, and so is the rolling row.

## Approach
```java
long[] prev = first row;
for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    long[] cur = new long[n];
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        long best = prev[j];
        if (j > 0) best = Math.min(best, prev[j - 1]);
        if (j < n - 1) best = Math.min(best, prev[j + 1]);
        cur[j] = m[i][j] + best;
    }
    prev = cur;
}
return min(prev);
```

## Why a new row array
Updating `prev` in place would overwrite `prev[j − 1]` before `cur[j]` reads it. A fresh array (or a saved diagonal value) keeps the previous row intact.

## Walkthrough (Example 1)
Rows `2 1 3`, `6 5 4`, `7 8 9`. Second row becomes `7 6 5`; third becomes `13 13 14`. Minimum 13, e.g. 1 → 5 → 7.

