## The one thing this teaches
**Edit-distance DPs change costs, not structure.** Swapping "1 per operation" for "the character's code" leaves the table and its transitions intact — only the numbers added along the edges change.

## Approach
```java
int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][m + 1];
for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) dp[i][m] = dp[i + 1][m] + s.charAt(i);
for (int j = m - 1; j >= 0; j--) dp[n][j] = dp[n][j + 1] + t.charAt(j);
for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--)
    for (int j = m - 1; j >= 0; j--)
        dp[i][j] = s.charAt(i) == t.charAt(j) ? dp[i + 1][j + 1]
                 : Math.min(s.charAt(i) + dp[i + 1][j], t.charAt(j) + dp[i][j + 1]);
return dp[0][0];
```

## Keep what is heavy
Equivalently, keep the common subsequence with the largest ASCII sum; everything else is deleted from both strings. The cost is `total(s) + total(t) − 2 × kept`.

## Walkthrough: sea / eat
Keep `ea` (weight 101 + 97). Delete `s` (115) and `t` (116): 231.

