## The one thing this teaches
**Skip duplicates at the same decision level, not across levels.** Choosing "the next element" from position `start` onward, two equal values offered at the same level lead to identical subtrees. The second copy is still allowed *deeper* — that is how `[2, 2]` is formed.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(a);

void build(int start, List<Integer> cur) {
    output(cur);                                   // every node of the tree is a subset
    for (int i = start; i < n; i++) {
        if (i > start && a[i] == a[i - 1]) continue;   // same value at the same level
        cur.add(a[i]);
        build(i + 1, cur);
        cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
    }
}
```

## Why the output is sorted
The tree is visited in preorder, a subset is printed before its extensions, and children are tried in increasing value — exactly lexicographic order on sorted sequences.

## Walkthrough: 1 2 2
`[]`, `[1]`, `[1 2]`, `[1 2 2]`, `[2]`, `[2 2]`. At the top level the second 2 is skipped; inside `[2]` it is used.

