## The one thing this teaches
**Carry only the remainder, and let pigeonhole bound the search.** The repunits grow without limit, but their remainders mod k live in k values. Each remainder determines the next, so once one repeats the sequence is in a cycle.

## Approach
```java
if (k % 2 == 0 || k % 5 == 0) return -1;   // a number ending in 1 is odd and not divisible by 5
int r = 0;
for (int len = 1; len <= k; len++) {
    r = (r * 10 + 1) % k;
    if (r == 0) return len;
}
return -1;
```

## Why k steps suffice
Among the first k + 1 remainders two must be equal. If 0 has not appeared by then, the sequence has entered a loop that does not contain 0.

## Why the shortcut is safe
Repunits end in 1, so they are odd and not multiples of 5. For every other k, a repunit divisible by k always exists — the loop will find it.

