## The one thing this teaches
**When values are small, count instead of compare.** A custom comparator works in O(n log n). With values below 1001, a counting array does the ordering for free — first in the order arr2 dictates, then in natural order.

## Approach
```java
int[] count = new int[1001];
for (int x : arr1) count[x]++;
List<Integer> out = new ArrayList<>();
for (int x : arr2) { while (count[x]-- > 0) out.add(x); }
for (int v = 0; v <= 1000; v++) while (count[v]-- > 0) out.add(v);
```

## The comparator version
Key each value by `(rank in arr2, value)` with rank = arr2's size for values not in arr2. Sorting by that pair gives the same order and works for any value range.

## The post-decrement trap
`count[x]-- > 0` leaves `count[x]` at −1 when it stops, which is harmless here since the second loop also stops at non-positive counts.

