long[] pow2 = powers of 2 mod MOD up to n;
long count = 0;
int l = 0, r = n - 1;
while (l <= r) {
    if (a[l] + a[r] > target) r--;               // a[r] is too big to pair with a[l] or anything after
    else { count = (count + pow2[r - l]) % MOD; l++; }
}
```

## Why sorting is allowed
A subsequence is a set of indices; its minimum and maximum do not depend on order. Sorting permutes the indices without changing how many sets satisfy the condition.

## Singletons
When `l == r`, `2a[l] ≤ target` and `pow2[0] = 1` counts the one-element subsequence `[a[l]]`.

