## The one thing this teaches
**Palindromes pair things up, with one exception in the middle.** Mirror symmetry means everything off-centre has a partner. Count the pairs greedily, then check whether a self-symmetric piece is left over for the centre.

## Approach
```java
Map<String, Integer> waiting = new HashMap<>();
int length = 0;
for (String w : words) {
    String rev = "" + w.charAt(1) + w.charAt(0);
    if (waiting.getOrDefault(rev, 0) > 0) { waiting.merge(rev, -1, Integer::sum); length += 4; }
    else waiting.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum);
}
for (entry : waiting) if (entry is like "gg" && entry.count > 0) { length += 2; break; }
```

## Why only one middle word
Two unpaired words in the middle would sit side by side and would have to mirror each other — but then they form a pair and were already counted.

## Walkthrough: lc cl gg
`cl` pairs with the waiting `lc` (+4). `gg` has no partner but is symmetric: +2. Total 6, e.g. `lcggcl`.

