## The one thing this teaches
**Encode tie-breaks in the comparison key.** "Fewer soldiers, then smaller index" is a pair compared lexicographically. Once the key is right, sorting or a heap does the rest.

## Approach
```java
PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((x, y) ->
    x[0] != y[0] ? y[0] - x[0] : y[1] - x[1]);        // max-heap: strongest on top
for (int i = 0; i < r; i++) {
    heap.add(new int[]{soldiers(m[i]), i});
    if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();                 // drop the strongest
}
// pop all k and reverse to list weakest first
```

## Counting with binary search
```java
int soldiers(int[] row) {
    int lo = 0, hi = row.length;
    while (lo < hi) { int mid = (lo + hi) / 2; if (row[mid] == 1) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid; }
    return lo;                                         // index of the first 0
}
```

## Why a max-heap for the smallest k
The heap keeps the k best candidates so far; the one to evict is the worst of them, which is exactly what a max-heap exposes.

