## The one thing this teaches
**When every action has the same reward, compare by cost alone.** Score changes by exactly 1 either way, so the only question is how much power moves — spend the least, receive the most. Sorting puts both extremes at the ends of the array.

## Approach
```java
Arrays.sort(tokens);
int lo = 0, hi = n - 1, score = 0, best = 0;
while (lo <= hi) {
    if (power >= tokens[lo]) { power -= tokens[lo++]; best = Math.max(best, ++score); }
    else if (score > 0 && lo < hi) { power += tokens[hi--]; score--; }
    else break;
}
return best;
```

## Why track the best
A final face-down play can lower the score for power that is never used. The answer is the highest score reached, not the last one.

## Why `lo < hi` for face down
Trading the very last token down gains power with nothing left to spend it on — it can only lose a point.

