## The one thing this teaches
**Precompute what a small domain allows.** Digits range over 0–9, so every factorial the loop will ever need fits in a table of ten entries. Looking one up is cheaper and clearer than a nested loop.

## Approach
```java
long[] fact = new long[10];
fact[0] = 1;
for (int d = 1; d < 10; d++) fact[d] = fact[d - 1] * d;
long sum = 0;
for (long v = n; v > 0; v /= 10) sum += fact[(int) (v % 10)];
return sum == n;
```

## 0! = 1
A zero digit contributes 1, not 0. `40585 = 4! + 0! + 5! + 8! + 5!` depends on it.

## There are only four
In base 10 the strong numbers are 1, 2, 145 and 40585. A 10-digit number's digit factorials sum to at most 10 · 9! = 3 628 800, far too small.

