## The one thing this teaches
**Characterise the shape instead of searching for it.** Rather than finding the rotation, describe what any rotation looks like: a single "drop" when the array is read in a circle. Counting drops is one pass.

## Approach
```java
int drops = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    if (a[i] > a[(i + 1) % n]) drops++;
return drops <= 1;
```

## Why the wrap-around pair is needed
`2 1 3 4` has one internal drop (2 > 1), but reading on from 4 back to 2 is fine only if 4 ≤ 2 — it is not, so there is a second drop and the answer is `NO`.

## Zero rotation
An already sorted array has no internal drop and one at the wrap (last > first), unless all values are equal — either way at most one.

