## The one thing this teaches
**BFS order is distance order.** Cells come out of the queue in non-decreasing distance, so the first exit dequeued is a nearest one — there is no need to explore the rest of the maze.

## Approach
```java
queue.add(entrance); seen[entrance] = true; dist[entrance] = 0;
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    cell = queue.poll();
    if (cell != entrance && onBorder(cell)) return dist[cell];
    for (each open, unseen neighbour) { seen = true; dist = dist[cell] + 1; queue.add(neighbour); }
}
return -1;
```

## The entrance on the border
The entrance may itself be a border cell, but it does not count as an exit — hence the explicit `cell != entrance` check.

## Checking at enqueue time
Testing for an exit when a neighbour is *enqueued* returns one level earlier and is equally correct; checking at dequeue time is simpler to get right.

