## The one thing this teaches
**Simulate the simple infinite process and filter.** Clipping the spiral against the grid edges case by case is fiddly. Walking the ideal spiral and ignoring out-of-bounds positions is short and obviously correct.

## Approach
```java
int[][] dirs = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {-1, 0}};   // E, S, W, N
record(r, c);
int d = 0, len = 1;
while (recorded < rows * cols) {
    for (int turn = 0; turn < 2; turn++) {            // two directions per length
        for (int s = 0; s < len; s++) {
            r += dirs[d][0]; c += dirs[d][1];
            if (inside(r, c)) record(r, c);
        }
        d = (d + 1) % 4;
    }
    len++;
}
```

## Why each cell is recorded once
The spiral never revisits a position: each ring lies strictly outside the previous one.

## Stopping mid-segment
The loop may finish a segment after the last cell is recorded; those extra steps are all outside the grid and record nothing.

