editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Maintain the reverse index, and keep both directions consistent.** Queries go from number to indices, updates from index to number. Two maps serve both — but every update must fix both, including the entry the old value leaves behind.

## Approach
```java
Map<Integer, Integer> at = new HashMap<>();
Map<Integer, TreeSet<Integer>> where = new HashMap<>();

void change(int index, int number) {
    Integer old = at.put(index, number);
    if (old != null) where.get(old).remove(index);            // stale entry
    where.computeIfAbsent(number, k -> new TreeSet<>()).add(index);
}

int find(int number) {
    TreeSet<Integer> s = where.get(number);
    return s == null || s.isEmpty() ? -1 : s.first();
}
```

## The heap alternative
A min-heap per number with **lazy deletion** also works: on find, pop indices whose current number (checked in `at`) no longer matches. Cheaper updates, occasionally slower queries.

## Same number again
`change 1 10` twice removes index 1 from 10's set and adds it back — harmless.

