## The one thing this teaches
**Go backwards when a choice jumps forward.** Solving question i affects which questions come *after* it. Defining the state as "best from i onward" makes that jump a lookup into an already-computed entry.

## Approach
```java
long[] dp = new long[n + 1];
for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    int next = Math.min(n, i + q[i][1] + 1);
    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i + 1], q[i][0] + dp[next]);
}
return dp[0];
```

## Why forwards is awkward
A forward state "best total having reached i" would need to know which earlier solved question still blocks i. The backward state never needs the past.

## Walkthrough (Example 1)
Questions `(3,2) (4,3) (4,4) (2,5)`: dp from the back is 2, 4, 4, 5. Solving question 0 skips 1 and 2 and then takes question 3: 3 + 2 = 5.

