## The one thing this teaches
**Find the case that breaks the invariant, and handle only that case slowly.** Rotated binary search depends on telling which half is sorted. Only the all-equal triple makes that impossible; every other step still halves the range.

## Approach
```java
int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;
while (lo <= hi) {
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == target) return true;
    if (a[lo] == a[mid] && a[mid] == a[hi]) { lo++; hi--; }
    else if (a[lo] <= a[mid]) {                      // left half sorted
        if (a[lo] <= target && target < a[mid]) hi = mid - 1; else lo = mid + 1;
    } else {                                         // right half sorted
        if (a[mid] < target && target <= a[hi]) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid - 1;
    }
}
return false;
```

## Why dropping both ends is safe
Both equal `a[mid]`, which is not the target. So neither end is the target either.

## Compared with the minimum problem
Finding the minimum compares only with `a[hi]`; searching for a value needs both ends to decide which half is sorted.

