## The one thing this teaches
**Binary search a derived monotone quantity.** The array values themselves are not what you search for. The *number missing so far*, `a[i] − (i + 1)`, is monotone, and the answer sits just before the first index where it reaches k.

## Approach
```java
int lo = 0, hi = n;                          // first i with missing(i) >= k, or n
while (lo < hi) {
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    if (a[mid] - (mid + 1) >= k) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
}
return (long) k + lo;
```

## Why k + lo
The answer is larger than exactly `lo` array values (those before index `lo`) and is the k-th number not in the array, so it is the `(k + lo)`-th positive integer.

