## The one thing this teaches
**Represent an infinite structure by its difference from a simple one.** The set is `{next, next + 1, …}` plus a few returned numbers below `next`. Those two pieces are finite and support both operations directly.

## Approach
```java
int next = 1;
TreeSet<Integer> returned = new TreeSet<>();

int pop() {
    if (!returned.isEmpty()) return returned.pollFirst();   // always smaller than next
    return next++;
}

void add(int x) {
    if (x < next) returned.add(x);     // x ≥ next is already present
}
```

## Why returned numbers are smaller than next
`add` only stores `x < next`, and `next` only grows — so everything in `returned` stays below it.

## Duplicates
A set ignores a second `add` of the same number. With a heap, keep a separate membership set to avoid pushing duplicates.

