## The one thing this teaches
**Carry the context a question needs down the traversal.** A node does not know its depth or parent, but its caller does. Passing `(node, parent, depth)` together records both facts in one pass.

## Approach
```java
ArrayDeque<Object[]> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
st.push(new Object[]{root, null, 0});
while (!st.isEmpty()) {
    Object[] e = st.pop();
    TreeNode t = (TreeNode) e[0];
    if (t.val == x) { px = (TreeNode) e[1]; dx = (int) e[2]; }
    if (t.val == y) { py = (TreeNode) e[1]; dy = (int) e[2]; }
    push children with (child, t, depth + 1);
}
return dx == dy && px != py;
```

## Siblings are not cousins
In `1 2 3`, nodes 2 and 3 are at the same depth but share the parent 1.

## The BFS view
In level-order traversal, check each level: both values present, and not as two children of the same node.

