## The one thing this teaches
**Look for the invariant under a messy process.** The smashing order seems to matter, but the final weight is always a signed sum of the original weights — one group minus the other. The process disappears and a subset-sum problem remains.

## Approach
```java
int total = sum(a);
boolean[] reach = new boolean[total / 2 + 1];
reach[0] = true;
for (int w : a)
    for (int s = total / 2; s >= w; s--)      // downward: each stone once
        reach[s] |= reach[s - w];
for (int s = total / 2; ; s--) if (reach[s]) return total - 2 * s;
```

## Why every split is achievable
Repeatedly smash a stone from the heavier group with one from the lighter group; the difference stays with the heavier group. When one group empties, the other holds the difference.

## Walkthrough: 2 7 4 1 8 1
Total 23. A subset summing to 11 exists (2 + 8 + 1), so the answer is 23 − 22 = 1.

