## The one thing this teaches
**Converging pointers can filter as they go.** The reversal pattern stays the same; each pointer just skips the positions that do not take part.

## Approach
```java
char[] c = s.toCharArray();
int lo = 0, hi = c.length - 1;
while (lo < hi) {
    while (lo < hi && !isVowel(c[lo])) lo++;
    while (lo < hi && !isVowel(c[hi])) hi--;
    char t = c[lo]; c[lo] = c[hi]; c[hi] = t;
    lo++; hi--;
}
return new String(c);
```

## Uppercase vowels
`A E I O U` count too — a lookup string such as `"aeiouAEIOU"` covers both cases.

## Why `lo < hi` inside the skips
Without it, a pointer could run past the other when the middle has no vowels, and the final swap would undo an earlier one.

