## The one thing this teaches
**Process a BFS queue one level at a time.** Recording the queue size before draining it separates the levels without storing depths.

## Approach
```java
long best = Long.MIN_VALUE;
int bestLevel = 0, level = 0;
Deque<TreeNode> q = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(root));
while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    level++;
    long sum = 0;
    for (int size = q.size(); size > 0; size--) {
        TreeNode t = q.poll();
        sum += t.val;
        if (t.left != null) q.add(t.left);
        if (t.right != null) q.add(t.right);
    }
    if (sum > best) { best = sum; bestLevel = level; }
}
```

## Negative sums
In a tree whose values are all negative, starting `best` at 0 would never update and report level 0.

## The DFS alternative
A DFS that passes the depth and adds each value to `sums[depth]` gives the same per-level totals.

