## The one thing this teaches
**Subsets of small sets are bitmasks, and so are sets of letters.** The search is over subsets of words; the constraint is on sets of letters. Representing both as bits makes each check a single AND.

## Approach
```java
List<Integer> masks = new ArrayList<>();          // words with distinct letters only
int best = 0;
void dfs(int i, int used) {
    best = Math.max(best, Integer.bitCount(used));
    for (int j = i; j < masks.size(); j++)
        if ((masks.get(j) & used) == 0) dfs(j + 1, used | masks.get(j));
}
```

## Length from the mask
Since no letter repeats, the concatenation's length is the number of set bits in `used` — no strings need to be built.

## The iterative version
Start with a list `[0]`. For each valid word mask, append `m | w` for every existing mask `m` disjoint from `w`. The list holds every reachable letter set.

