## The one thing this teaches
**Transitive grouping is connected components.** "Similar" is not transitive — `tars ~ rats ~ arts`, yet `tars` and `arts` differ in more than a swap. Groups are the closure of the relation, which is what union-find computes.

## Approach
```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
        if (similar(words[i], words[j]) && union(i, j)) groups--;

boolean similar(String a, String b) {
    int diff = 0;
    for (int k = 0; k < a.length(); k++)
        if (a.charAt(k) != b.charAt(k) && ++diff > 2) return false;
    return diff == 0 || diff == 2;
}
```

## Why two differences mean one swap
The words are anagrams, so if they differ in exactly positions i and j, the letters there must be `(x, y)` and `(y, x)` — a swap.

## Stopping early
The comparison returns as soon as a third difference appears, which keeps most pair checks far below O(L).

