## The one thing this teaches
**Let the loop run to the longer length and guard each read.** Instead of a main loop plus two cleanup loops, one index over the longer length with a bounds check per string handles every case.

## Approach
```java
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i < Math.max(a.length(), b.length()); i++) {
    if (i < a.length()) sb.append(a.charAt(i));
    if (i < b.length()) sb.append(b.charAt(i));
}
```

## Why a StringBuilder
`result += ch` in a loop copies the whole string each time — O(n²) total. A builder appends in amortised O(1).

## Order within a round
The first string's character always comes first, including in the last round when both still have characters.

