## The one thing this teaches
**Reduce a simulation to a property of each element.** Each bulb's final state depends only on how many times it is toggled, which is its number of divisors — and divisor counts are odd exactly for perfect squares.

## Approach
```java
long lo = 0, hi = 1_000_000_000L;           // largest r with r·r ≤ n
while (lo < hi) {
    long mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2;
    if (mid * mid <= n) lo = mid; else hi = mid - 1;
}
return lo;
```

## Why not Math.sqrt
`(long) Math.sqrt(n)` can be off by one for n near 10^18, where a double cannot represent every integer. An integer binary search is exact.

## Walkthrough: n = 10
Squares 1, 4, 9 have divisors {1}, {1, 2, 4}, {1, 3, 9}. Every other bulb has an even count. Answer 3 = ⌊√10⌋.

