## The one thing this teaches
**Longest-first greedy for partitions with a hereditary rule.** If a substring has unique letters, so does every part of it. So cutting as late as possible never forces extra cuts later.

## Approach
```java
int pieces = 1, mask = 0;
for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
    int bit = 1 << (ch - 'a');
    if ((mask & bit) != 0) { pieces++; mask = 0; }   // repeat: cut before ch
    mask |= bit;
}
return pieces;
```

## Why greedy is optimal
Compare with any valid partition. By induction, each greedy cut is at or after the other partition's corresponding cut — the greedy piece is the longest possible from the same start or a later one.

## Walkthrough: abacaba
`ab` | `ac` | `ab` | `a` — each cut happens when an `a` repeats: 4 pieces.

