## The one thing this teaches
**Enumerate palindromes by their centre, not by their ends.** Choosing both ends gives O(n²) candidates that each need an O(n) check. Choosing a centre and growing outwards checks each candidate in O(1), because the inner part was confirmed one step earlier.

## Approach
```java
long count = 0;
for (int c = 0; c < 2 * n - 1; c++) {
    int l = c / 2, r = l + c % 2;          // even c: a letter; odd c: a gap
    while (l >= 0 && r < n && s.charAt(l) == s.charAt(r)) {
        count++;
        l--; r++;
    }
}
```

## Why the gaps matter
`abba` has no middle letter. Expanding only from letters finds `a`, `b`, `b`, `a` and misses `bb` and `abba`.

## Beyond this
The same loop, keeping the longest expansion instead of counting, is *longest palindromic substring*. Manacher's algorithm makes it O(n) by reusing mirror images, and is rarely expected in an interview.

