## The one thing this teaches
**The O(n log n) lower bound is about comparisons.** A sort that uses each value as an index learns more than a comparison does, and when the value range k is small it is O(n + k).

## Approach
```java
int[] count = new int[151];
for (int x : a) count[x]++;
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int age = 0; age <= 150; age++)
    for (int c = 0; c < count[age]; c++) sb.append(age).append(' ');
```

## When it stops working
Counting sort needs an array of size k. For ages k = 151 is nothing; for arbitrary 32-bit integers k is four billion. Radix sort is the fix: counting sort applied one digit at a time.

## Output is part of the cost
With n up to 10^6, building the output with string `+=` is quadratic. Use a `StringBuilder`.

