## The one thing this teaches
**Reduce after every multiplication, and before the first one.** `(x · y) mod p = ((x mod p) · (y mod p)) mod p`, so you may reduce whenever you like — and you must reduce often enough that no intermediate product overflows.

## Approach
```java
static final long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;

long result = 1 % MOD, base = a % MOD;         // reduce the base first
while (b > 0) {
    if ((b & 1) == 1) result = result * base % MOD;
    base = base * base % MOD;
    b >>= 1;
}
return result;
```

## Why a `long` is enough
Both factors are below 10^9 + 7 < 2^30, so the product is below 2^60 < 2^63. Skip `a % MOD` and the first `base * base` is (10^18)², which overflows silently.

## Where this is used
Every "answer modulo 10^9 + 7" problem, and the modular inverse: by Fermat's little theorem `x^(p−2) mod p` is the inverse of `x` when `p` is prime.

