## The one thing this teaches
**Division mod a prime is multiplication by an inverse.** Mod p you cannot divide, but when p is prime every nonzero x has an inverse x^(p−2) (Fermat's little theorem), so `a / b` becomes `a · b^(p−2)`.

## Approach
```java
long[] fact = new long[M + 1], inv = new long[M + 1];
fact[0] = 1;
for (int i = 1; i <= M; i++) fact[i] = fact[i - 1] * i % MOD;
inv[M] = power(fact[M], MOD - 2);                 // one O(log p) inverse
for (int i = M; i > 0; i--) inv[i - 1] = inv[i] * i % MOD;   // the rest for free

long nCr(int n, int r) {
    if (r < 0 || r > n) return 0;
    return fact[n] * inv[r] % MOD * inv[n - r] % MOD;
}
```

## Why walk the inverses downwards
`1/(i−1)! = i / i!`, so one inverse at the top gives every other one by multiplication. Computing each inverse separately is O(M log p).

## Two bugs to avoid
- `fact[n] * inv[r] * inv[n - r] % MOD` overflows: reduce after *each* product.
- `R > N` must be 0, not an out-of-bounds read.

