## The one thing this teaches
**A splice needs both ends.** Inserting a list between two nodes updates four pointers, and two of them live at the inserted list's tail. Have the recursive flatten return that tail, and each splice becomes constant work.

## Approach
```java
// Flattens the list starting at h, in place; returns its last node.
static Node flatten(Node h) {
    Node p = h, last = h;
    while (p != null) {
        Node next = p.next;
        if (p.child != null) {
            Node tail = flatten(p.child);
            p.next = p.child;  p.child.prev = p;  p.child = null;
            tail.next = next;  if (next != null) next.prev = tail;
            last = tail;
        } else {
            last = p;
        }
        p = next;
    }
    return last;
}
```

## The three pointers people forget
- `child.prev = p` — the child head's `prev` was null.
- `p.child = null` — a flattened list with children left over is not flat.
- `next.prev = tail` — the old next now follows the child tail, not `p`.

