editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**Each traversal tells you something different.** Preorder tells you *which* node is the root; inorder tells you *what is on each side* of it. Neither alone determines the tree; together, with distinct values, they do.

## Approach
```java
int preIdx = 0;
Map<Integer, Integer> pos = new HashMap<>();   // value -> inorder index

TreeNode build(int[] pre, int lo, int hi) {          // inorder range [lo, hi]
    if (lo > hi) return null;
    TreeNode root = new TreeNode(pre[preIdx++]);
    int m = pos.get(root.val);
    root.left = build(pre, lo, m - 1);   // left first: preorder order
    root.right = build(pre, m + 1, hi);
    return root;
}
```

## Why left must be built first
The shared preorder index moves root → left subtree → right subtree. Building the right side first reads the left subtree's values as if they were the right's.

## The slow version
Searching the inorder for each root instead of using a map is O(n²) on a skewed tree.

starter_code.java:
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val;
        TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
    }

    static String level(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return "EMPTY";
        List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();
        LinkedList<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();   // LinkedList: ArrayDeque rejects null
        q.add(root);
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = q.poll();
            if (node == null) { out.add("null"); continue; }
            out.add(String.valueOf(node.val));
            q.add(node.left);
            q.add(node.right);
        }
        while (!out.isEmpty() && out.get(out.size() - 1).equals("null")) out.remove(out.size() - 1);
        return String.join(" ", out);
    }

    static TreeNode solve(int[] pre, int[] ino) {
        // TODO: the next preorder value is the root; its inorder position splits the range into left and right subtrees
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] pre = new int[n], ino = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) pre[i] = sc.nextInt();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) ino[i] = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.println(level(solve(pre, ino)));
    }
}

