editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**A window you need to *search* is an ordered set.** A hash set answers "is this exact value in the window?"; the question here is "is any value *close*?", and that is a `ceiling` query.

## Approach
```java
TreeSet<Long> window = new TreeSet<>();
for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
    long x = a[j];
    Long c = window.ceiling(x - t);           // smallest value >= x - t
    if (c != null && c <= x + t) return true;
    window.add(x);
    if (j >= k) window.remove((long) a[j - k]);  // keep only the last k
}
return false;
```

## Two traps
- **Overflow.** `x - t` with x near −2^31 and t up to 2·10^9 does not fit in `int`. Work in `long`.
- **Duplicates.** If the window already holds x, `ceiling` finds it and returns true before the add — so a set is enough.

## O(n) with buckets
Buckets of width t + 1 hold at most one window value each (two would already be an answer), and a match can only be in the same bucket or a neighbour.

