## The one thing this teaches
**Ask a spanning-tree question by changing the input and re-running.** "Is this edge in every MST?" has no direct formula, but "what is the best tree without it?" and "what is the best tree that is forced to use it?" are each one Kruskal pass.

## Approach
```java
int base = kruskal(-1, -1);             // (skip, force)
for (int e = 0; e < m; e++) {
    if (kruskal(e, -1) > base) critical.add(e);            // needed by every MST
    else if (kruskal(-1, e) == base) pseudo.add(e);     // usable by some MST
}

int kruskal(int skip, int force) {
    DSU d = new DSU(n);
    int w = 0, used = 0;
    if (force >= 0) { d.union(u[force], v[force]); w += wt[force]; used++; }
    for (int e : byWeight) {                          // indices sorted by weight, once
        if (e == skip) continue;
        if (d.union(u[e], v[e])) { w += wt[e]; used++; }
    }
    return used == n - 1 ? w : Integer.MAX_VALUE;     // disconnected = infinitely heavy
}
```

## Why the order of the two tests matters
A critical edge also passes the forced test (forcing an edge every MST uses changes nothing), so check critical first.

## Sort once
The edge order does not change between runs; sorting inside `kruskal` turns O(m²) into O(m² log m).

