## The one thing this teaches
**Floyd–Warshall is a DP over which nodes a path may pass through.** After the k-th outer iteration, `dist[i][j]` is the best path that uses only nodes 0 … k as stops. Allowing node k as well either improves a path by going through it, or it does not.

## Approach
```java
long INF = Long.MAX_VALUE / 4;
for (long[] row : dist) Arrays.fill(row, INF);
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) dist[i][i] = 0;
for (int[] e : edges) dist[e[0]][e[1]] = Math.min(dist[e[0]][e[1]], e[2]);   // repeated edges

for (int k = 0; k < n; k++)                 // k OUTERMOST
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (dist[i][k] == INF) continue;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
            if (dist[k][j] != INF && dist[i][k] + dist[k][j] < dist[i][j])
                dist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j];
    }
```

## The loop-order bug
With k innermost, `dist[i][j]` is finalised before the paths through later nodes have been computed. The code still runs and gives wrong answers.

## When to use it
n up to a few hundred and all pairs needed. For one source, Dijkstra (non-negative) or Bellman-Ford (negative) is far cheaper.

