## The one thing this teaches
**When a choice consumes a resource, the amount left is a dimension of the state.** Greedy by value-per-weight is optimal only when items can be split. Whole items need `best[c]` for every capacity `c`.

## Approach
```java
long[] best = new long[W + 1];               // best value within capacity c
for (int[] item : items) {
    int w = item[0], v = item[1];
    for (int c = W; c >= w; c--)                  // DOWNWARDS: each item once
        best[c] = Math.max(best[c], best[c - w] + v);
}
return best[W];
```

## Why downwards
Going upwards, `best[c − w]` may already include this item, so the same item gets packed again — that is the *unbounded* knapsack. The loop direction is the whole difference between the two problems.

## The table this rolls up
`dp[i][c] = max(dp[i−1][c], dp[i−1][c−w] + v)`. Row i reads only row i − 1, so one row is enough if you do not overwrite what you still need.

