## The one thing this teaches
**Binary search on the answer, with a linear check.** "Is there a repeat of length L?" is monotone, so the longest L takes log n checks — and a rolling hash makes each check O(n) instead of O(n·L).

## Approach
```java
int lo = 1, hi = n - 1;
String best = null;
while (lo <= hi) {
    int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
    String r = repeatOfLength(s, mid);    // smallest verified repeat, or null
    if (r != null) { best = r; lo = mid + 1; } else hi = mid - 1;
}
```

## The rolling check
Hash every window of length L with `h = h·B + c mod P`, dropping the outgoing character with `− c·B^(L−1)`. Group start indices by hash; within a group, compare real substrings before believing a match.

## Why verify
A polynomial hash mod 10^9 + 7 over 3·10^4 windows will usually be collision-free — and "usually" is not a proof. Verifying keeps the answer exact at an expected cost that stays linear.

## The heavyweight alternative
A suffix array with LCP gives O(n log n) deterministically. It is rarely expected in an interview; the binary search is.

