editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**A segment tree works for any associative combine, not only sums.** A Fenwick tree needs an inverse (sums subtract), and minimum has none. A segment tree never subtracts: it covers `[l, r]` exactly with O(log n) stored ranges and combines them.

## Approach (iterative, bottom-up)
```java
int size = n;
long[] t = new long[2 * size];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) t[size + i] = a[i];
for (int p = size - 1; p > 0; p--) t[p] = Math.min(t[2 * p], t[2 * p + 1]);

void set(int i, long v) {
    for (t[i += size] = v; i > 1; i >>= 1) t[i >> 1] = Math.min(t[i], t[i ^ 1]);
}

long min(int l, int r) {                       // inclusive
    long res = Long.MAX_VALUE;
    for (l += size, r += size + 1; l < r; l >>= 1, r >>= 1) {
        if ((l & 1) == 1) res = Math.min(res, t[l++]);
        if ((r & 1) == 1) res = Math.min(res, t[--r]);
    }
    return res;
}
```

## Picking the structure
| Need | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Range sum, point update | Fenwick |
| Range min/max/gcd, point update | Segment tree |
| Range update and range query | Segment tree with lazy propagation (or two Fenwicks for sums) |
| No updates at all | Prefix sums, or a sparse table for min |

