## The one thing this teaches
**Change what you store until the operation becomes a point update.** A range add is two point updates to a difference array; a range sum over a difference array is a weighted prefix sum; and a weighted prefix sum is two ordinary ones.

## The algebra
With `a[i] = Σ_{j≤i} d[j]`:

`prefix(i) = Σ_{j≤i} d[j]·(i + 1 − j) = (i + 1)·Σ d[j] − Σ d[j]·j`

## Approach
```java
long[] b1 = new long[n + 1], b2 = new long[n + 1];

void rangeAdd(int l, int r, long v) {
    add(b1, l, v);        add(b1, r + 1, -v);
    add(b2, l, v * l);    add(b2, r + 1, -v * (r + 1));
}

long prefix(int i) {
    return query(b1, i) * (i + 1) - query(b2, i);
}
```

Load the initial array as n range adds of length one, or keep its plain prefix sums alongside.

## When to reach for a lazy segment tree instead
When the update or query is not a sum — "set every value in a range", or range add with range minimum.

