## The one thing this teaches
**Some search problems have a counting answer.** Euler's theorem replaces a search over stroke orders with two checks: degrees and connectivity.

## Why degrees
Each pass through a vertex consumes one edge in and one edge out, so interior visits use edges in pairs. A vertex with odd degree must therefore be where the stroke starts or ends — and there are only two ends. (With 0 odd vertices the stroke is a closed circuit.)

## Approach
```java
int odd = 0;
for (int v = 0; v < n; v++) if (deg[v] % 2 == 1) odd++;
if (odd != 0 && odd != 2) return false;
// every vertex with deg > 0 must be reachable from any one of them
return allEdgesInOneComponent(adj, deg);
```

## The connectivity trap
Two separate triangles have every degree even, and still cannot be drawn in one stroke. Check that the vertices with edges form one component — and ignore the isolated ones, which have nothing to draw.

## Actually drawing it
Hierholzer's algorithm finds the stroke in O(m); *Rebuild the Itinerary* uses it.

