editorial:
## The one thing this teaches
**One DFS knows which subtrees can escape.** `low[v]` is the earliest-discovered vertex that v's subtree can reach using tree edges down and one back edge up. If a child's subtree cannot get above u, removing u cuts it off.

## Approach
```java
void dfs(int u, int parent) {
    disc[u] = low[u] = ++timer;
    int children = 0;
    for (int v : adj[u]) {
        if (v == parent) continue;
        if (disc[v] != 0) { low[u] = Math.min(low[u], disc[v]); continue; }   // back edge
        children++;
        dfs(v, u);
        low[u] = Math.min(low[u], low[v]);
        if (parent != -1 && low[v] >= disc[u]) critical[u] = true;
    }
    if (parent == -1 && children > 1) critical[u] = true;           // root rule
}
```

## Why the root is special
The root has nothing above it, so `low[v] ≥ disc[root]` always holds. It is critical exactly when the DFS had to start two separate subtrees from it.

## Bridges are the same DFS
An *edge* u–v is a bridge when `low[v] > disc[u]` — strictly greater, because v's subtree cannot even reach u without that edge.

