## The one thing this teaches
**A component is found when its root finishes.** In the DFS, the root of a strongly connected component is the first of its vertices visited. Nothing below it can reach anything visited earlier and still on the stack, so `low[root] == disc[root]` — and the component is exactly the stack above it.

## Approach
```java
void dfs(int u) {
    disc[u] = low[u] = ++timer;
    stack.push(u); onStack[u] = true;
    for (int v : adj[u]) {
        if (disc[v] == 0) { dfs(v); low[u] = Math.min(low[u], low[v]); }
        else if (onStack[v]) low[u] = Math.min(low[u], disc[v]);   // only unassigned vertices count
    }
    if (low[u] == disc[u]) {                      // u is a root
        int x;
        do { x = stack.pop(); onStack[x] = false; } while (x != u);
        components++;
    }
}
```

## Why `onStack`
An edge to a vertex already assigned to an *earlier* component is a one-way street out; it must not lower `low[u]`. Checking only `disc[v] != 0` merges components that are not mutually reachable.

## Kosaraju, for comparison
DFS once to record finish order; reverse every edge; DFS again in decreasing finish order. Each second-pass tree is a component. Two passes, but no low-links.

