## The one thing this teaches
**The integers 0 … 2^n − 1 are the subsets of n items.** No recursion and no list-building: bit i of the mask says whether item i is in. This is the representation every bitmask DP starts from.

## Approach
```java
long total = 0;
for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {
    int x = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        if ((mask >> i & 1) == 1) x ^= a[i];
    total += x;
}
```

## The O(n) shortcut
A bit is set in the OR of all elements exactly when some element has it — and then it is set in exactly half of the 2^n subset XORs. So the total is `(a[0] | … | a[n−1]) · 2^(n−1)`. Worth knowing; the enumeration is the transferable skill.

## When enumeration stops working
2^20 ≈ 10^6 is fine; 2^30 is not. "n ≤ 20" in the constraints is the signal for bitmasks.

