## The one thing this teaches
**When where you have been matters, put it in the BFS state.** Shortest paths need states whose futures do not depend on the past. The room alone is not such a state; the room plus the visited set is.

## Approach
```java
int full = (1 << n) - 1;
int[][] dist = new int[n][1 << n];
for (int[] row : dist) Arrays.fill(row, -1);
ArrayDeque<int[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) { dist[r][1 << r] = 0; q.add(new int[]{r, 1 << r}); }   // any start
while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    int[] s = q.poll();
    int u = s[0], mask = s[1];
    if (mask == full) return dist[u][mask];
    for (int v : adj[u]) {
        int next = mask | 1 << v;
        if (dist[v][next] == -1) {
            dist[v][next] = dist[u][mask] + 1;
            q.add(new int[]{v, next});
        }
    }
}
```

## Multi-source start
Putting every (room, just-that-room) state in the queue at distance 0 is the same trick as rotting oranges: one BFS answers "starting from anywhere".

## The TSP view
This is a shortest Hamiltonian *walk*: all-pairs distances plus `dp[mask][end]` gives the same answer, in O(2^n · n²).

